self_contained_problem:
A graph $G$ is called claw-free if it does not contain the complete bipartite graph $K_{1,3}$ as an induced subgraph. A graph is $k$-regular if every vertex has degree exactly $k$, and $k$-connected if it has more than $k$ vertices and remains connected after removal of any fewer than $k$ vertices. A Hamilton cycle in $G$ is a cycle that visits every vertex of $G$ exactly once.

Let $G$ be a finite simple graph satisfying all of the following:
(i) $G$ is 4-connected;
(ii) $G$ is 4-regular;
(iii) $G$ is claw-free;
(iv) every vertex of $G$ is contained in exactly two triangles of $G$.

Prove or disprove: every such graph $G$ admits a Hamilton cycle.

For a positive integer N, let T be a bounded linear operator presented as a 2×N partitioned block operator
T = [ A_1  A_2  ...  A_N ;
      B_1  B_2  ...  B_N ],
where each A_j, B_j is a bounded operator (acting between appropriate Hilbert spaces) belonging to the Schatten p-class. For 1 ≤ p < ∞, the Schatten p-norm of an operator X with singular values s_1(X) ≥ s_2(X) ≥ ... is defined by ||X||_p = (Σ_k s_k(X)^p)^{1/p}. Define the Schatten p-norm compression of T as the 2×N scalar matrix
C_p(T) = [ ||A_1||_p  ||A_2||_p  ...  ||A_N||_p ;
           ||B_1||_p  ||B_2||_p  ...  ||B_N||_p ].

Prove or disprove that for every such T:
(i) ||T||_p ≥ ||C_p(T)||_p for all 1 ≤ p ≤ 2;
(ii) ||T||_p ≤ ||C_p(T)||_p for all p ≥ 2,
where in each case ||C_p(T)||_p denotes the Schatten p-norm of the 2×N scalar matrix C_p(T).

Consider the magnetic-field fluctuations of the turbulent interplanetary solar wind. Let $\mathbf{B}_0$ denote the local background magnetic field and write the total field as $\mathbf{B}(\mathbf{x},t)=\mathbf{B}_0+\mathbf{b}(\mathbf{x},t)$, where $\mathbf{b}$ is the fluctuating component with characteristic wave vector $\mathbf{k}$. Several idealized decompositions of $\mathbf{b}$ have been proposed:

(i) a "slab" component of planar, parallel-propagating Alfvén waves, for which $\mathbf{k}\parallel\mathbf{B}_0$ and $\mathbf{b}\perp\mathbf{B}_0$;
(ii) a "quasi-two-dimensional" component for which both $\mathbf{k}\perp\mathbf{B}_0$ and $\mathbf{b}\perp\mathbf{B}_0$;
(iii) a population of highly refracted MHD waves whose wave vectors have been substantially deflected from their source directions by inhomogeneities;
(iv) pressure-balanced structures, i.e., quasi-static fluctuations satisfying $\delta(p_{\text{thermal}}+|\mathbf{B}|^2/(8\pi))\approx 0$;
(v) any mixture of (i)–(iv).

Determine the three-dimensional symmetry of solar-wind magnetic-field turbulence: which of the above components, or which weighted combination of them, correctly describes the observed fluctuations, and in particular establish whether the perpendicular component is genuinely quasi-two-dimensional (with $\mathbf{k}$ and $\mathbf{b}$ both perpendicular to $\mathbf{B}_0$) as opposed to consisting of highly refracted waves or pressure-balanced structures.

In the framework of general relativity, a spacetime is a smooth 4-dimensional Lorentzian manifold (M, g) satisfying the Einstein field equations. A black hole region is traditionally defined as the complement of the causal past of future null infinity, and its boundary is the event horizon; this definition is teleological, requiring knowledge of the entire future of the spacetime. To provide a quasi-local alternative, various notions such as trapped surfaces, apparent horizons, isolated horizons, marginally outer trapped surfaces (MOTS), and dynamical horizons have been proposed. A dynamical horizon is informally a smooth, spacelike 3-dimensional hypersurface H ⊂ M that admits a foliation by closed 2-surfaces S such that on each leaf S the expansion θ_(ℓ) of one (outgoing) null normal ℓ vanishes and the expansion θ_(n) of the other (ingoing) null normal n is strictly negative.

Problem. Formulate a precise and physically appropriate mathematical definition of a dynamical horizon in general relativity. The definition should:
(a) be fully quasi-local, i.e., depend only on data in an arbitrarily small neighborhood of the hypersurface, without reference to the global causal structure or to future null infinity;
(b) apply to genuinely time-dependent (non-stationary) black-hole spacetimes and reduce, in the stationary limit, to the standard notion of a Killing/event horizon;
(c) ensure existence, uniqueness, regularity, and stability properties (in particular, addressing non-uniqueness of MOTS foliations and the dependence on the choice of spacelike slicing);
(d) yield versions of the laws of black-hole mechanics (area increase, a first law, etc.) and be compatible with reasonable energy conditions;
(e) admit rigorous existence results in physically relevant classes of solutions of the Einstein equations.

Let $X$ be a smooth projective variety over $\mathbb{C}$ with pseudoeffective canonical bundle $K_X$ (i.e. $c_1(K_X)$ lies in the closure of the cone of classes of effective $\mathbb{R}$-divisors). On such an $X$ one can define the following positive measures:

(a) The canonical measure $d\mu_{\mathrm{can}}$, constructed (via the Iitaka fibration when $\kappa(X)\ge 0$) from the unique Kähler–Einstein-type metric of the canonical model;

(b) The maximal volume form $dV_{\max}$, defined as the upper envelope $\sup\{\,(i^{n^{2}}\Omega\wedge\bar\Omega)^{1/m}\,\}$ taken over all pluricanonical sections $\Omega\in H^{0}(X,mK_X)$ of $L^{2/m}$-norm $\le 1$, $m\ge 1$;

(c) Its non-pluripolar regularization $d\hat V_{\max}$ obtained by taking the non-pluripolar part of the Monge–Ampère current associated with the singular Hermitian metric on $K_X$ with minimal singularities;

(d) The closely related measure $d\hat V_{\mathrm{can}}$ produced from the canonical singular Hermitian metric on $K_X$.

Prove or disprove the following two assertions:

(1) The Kodaira dimension satisfies $\kappa(X)\ge 0$;

(2) There exists a constant $C>0$ such that
$$d\hat V_{\mathrm{can}}\;=\;d\hat V_{\max}\;=\;dV_{\max}\;=\;C\cdot d\mu_{\mathrm{can}}$$
as measures on $X$.

Consider the problem of invariant feature learning (IFL) for prediction under distribution shift. Let $\mathcal{X}$ be an input space, $\mathcal{Y}$ a label space, and $\mathcal{E}$ a set of environments. For each $e \in \mathcal{E}$ there is a distribution $P^e$ on $\mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{Y}$. The goal of IFL is to learn a representation $\Phi: \mathcal{X} \to \mathcal{Z}$ and a predictor $h: \mathcal{Z} \to \mathcal{Y}$ such that $h \circ \Phi$ has small risk simultaneously across all $e \in \mathcal{E}$, by separating features whose predictive relationship with $Y$ is invariant across environments from features whose relationship is spurious (i.e., varies across environments).

Standard IFL approaches rely on observing, for each training example, the pair $(x, y)$ together with an environment label $e$. The following types of additional supervision can be made available during training:

(a) a family of content-preserving transformations $\mathcal{T} = \{t: \mathcal{X} \to \mathcal{X}\}$ assumed to preserve the invariant content of $x$ while possibly altering spurious factors;
(b) a discrete environment label $e \in \mathcal{E}$;
(c) a high-dimensional environment descriptor $u^e \in \mathbb{R}^d$ characterizing each environment;
(d) richer per-example annotations such as object-part labels or attribute labels $a \in \mathcal{A}$ that describe semantic structure of $x$ beyond the class label $y$.

Problem: Design a learning procedure that uses any combination of the supervision signals (a)–(d) above and provably (or with quantitative guarantees) recovers a representation $\Phi$ for which the conditional distribution of $Y \mid \Phi(X)$ is invariant across environments, while discarding spurious predictive structure. Specifically:

1. Identify minimal sufficient conditions on the available supervision (a)–(d) under which the invariant predictor is identifiable from finite samples drawn from a finite collection of training environments.
2. Provide an algorithm combining these signals and bound its excess worst-case risk over an unseen test environment in terms of the strength and type of supervision used.
3. Determine which of (a)–(d), or which combinations, are strictly more informative than environment labels alone for separating invariant from spurious features.

Consider a decision-making system that, in addition to producing predictions, provides algorithmic recourse: for each individual receiving an unfavorable automated decision, the system specifies a set of feasible actions (changes to a subset of mutable features, possibly through a structural causal model of the world) that, if performed, would change the decision to a favorable one. Suppose individuals are partitioned into a protected group A=1 and an unprotected group A=0 according to a sensitive attribute A. Formulate a principled mathematical definition of fairness for such a recourse system that goes beyond fairness of the prediction itself. In particular, propose definitions and corresponding measurable criteria that capture:

(i) equality across the groups A=0 and A=1 of the probability that an individual who receives an unfavorable decision is offered a feasible recourse (the existence of recourse);

(ii) equality across groups of the cost or effort required to act on a recommended recourse, where cost is defined with respect to a specified action-cost function on the structural causal model (e.g., expected minimal intervention cost to flip the decision);

(iii) compatibility of these recourse-fairness criteria with standard prediction-fairness notions (such as demographic parity, equalized odds, or counterfactual fairness), including impossibility or trade-off theorems describing when these criteria can or cannot be simultaneously satisfied;

(iv) a normative criterion specifying under what conditions it is justified to perform recourse-prescribing interventions on all members of a subgroup rather than on individuals.

Provide such definitions, prove their basic properties (existence, identifiability from observational and interventional data, and behaviour under composition), and characterize the class of causal models and cost functions for which fair recourse in senses (i)–(ii) is achievable simultaneously with a prescribed prediction-fairness notion.

Consider the following software-engineering research problem. Let P be a program (or software system) under test, and let T = {t_1, ..., t_n} be an existing test suite for P, where each test t_i consists of a sequence of inputs/calls to P together with a set of assertions A(t_i) that check expected behavior. Suppose further that one has access to a historical fault corpus F = {(P_j, t_j*, d_j)} consisting of past versions of P (or related programs), tests t_j* that exposed defects, and descriptions d_j of those defects, as well as a catalogue E of common programmer-error patterns (e.g., off-by-one, null dereference, boundary conditions).

Design and evaluate a methodology that, using a large language model L (a pretrained autoregressive transformer over source code and natural language), takes (P, T, F, E) as input and produces an augmented test suite T' ⊇ T obtained by adding new assertions and/or new test cases, such that:

(a) T' strictly increases coverage of corner cases, historical fault patterns from F, and likely programmer errors from E, relative to T;
(b) the augmentation is achieved either via few-shot prompting of L (constructing demonstrations from T and F) or via fine-tuning of L on (T, F, E);
(c) added assertions are sound with respect to the intended behavior of P (no spurious failures on correct executions), measured on a held-out validation set;
(d) the procedure provides quantifiable improvements over baselines that generate test suites from scratch without leveraging T.

Concretely: provide an algorithm A_L for producing T' from (P, T, F, E), and establish empirical or theoretical guarantees on the fault-detection effectiveness, soundness rate, and assertion-coverage gain of T' compared with T and with state-of-the-art automated test generation baselines.

Consider a software-engineering process modeled as a directed workflow graph whose nodes represent activities such as specification, code generation, test generation, static analysis, dynamic analysis, response post-processing, code review, and continuous-integration (CI) build/test stages, and whose edges represent the flow of artifacts (specifications, code, tests, traces, reports) between activities. Suppose that one or more nodes are instantiated by a large language model (LLM), treated as a stochastic transducer that maps prompt-context inputs to natural-language or code outputs whose correctness, safety, and efficiency cannot be guaranteed a priori.

Identify and characterize design patterns and workflow architectures—i.e., reusable structural arrangements of nodes, edges, guards, validators, and feedback loops—that enable LLM nodes to be embedded into such a process so that the overall workflow:
(i) is safe, in the sense that outputs admitted into downstream stages satisfy specified soundness or validity constraints (for instance, type-correctness, test-passing, absence of identified vulnerability classes);
(ii) is efficient, in terms of bounded computational cost, latency, and number of LLM invocations per accepted artifact; and
(iii) is effective, in the sense of measurably improving an end-to-end objective (defect rate, coverage, repair success, developer productivity) compared with a baseline workflow without LLM nodes.

In particular, study the following workflow families and determine which architectural patterns provably or empirically meet criteria (i)–(iii):
(a) generate-and-test frameworks, where an LLM proposes candidate artifacts that are filtered by an oracle or test suite;
(b) pipelines integrating static and dynamic program analyses with prompt construction and post-processing of LLM outputs;
(c) post-processing stages that validate, repair, or reject LLM responses;
(d) continuous-integration pipelines extended with LLM-driven stages.

Provide a taxonomy of such patterns, criteria for selecting among them, and conditions under which each pattern guarantees the safety, efficiency, and effectiveness properties above.

Let $G$ be a discrete group and let $p$ be a prime number. The classical group von Neumann algebra $\mathcal{N}(G)$ is the algebra of $G$-equivariant bounded operators on $\ell^2(G)$; it contains the complex group ring $\mathbb{C}G$ and carries a faithful normal trace inducing a dimension function $\dim_{\mathcal{N}(G)}$ on (arbitrary) $\mathcal{N}(G)$-modules. Using this dimension, the $L^2$-Betti numbers $b_n^{(2)}(G;\mathbb{C}) = \dim_{\mathcal{N}(G)} H_n(G;\mathcal{N}(G))$ are defined, and Lück's approximation theorem states that for any residually finite $G$ with a normal chain $G = G_0 \supseteq G_1 \supseteq \cdots$ of finite-index normal subgroups with $\bigcap_i G_i = 1$, the normalized Betti numbers $b_n(G_i;\mathbb{Q})/[G:G_i]$ converge to $b_n^{(2)}(G;\mathbb{C})$.

Question: Does there exist an $\mathbb{F}_p$-algebra $\mathcal{N}(G;p)$ such that:
(a) $\mathcal{N}(G;p)$ contains the group ring $\mathbb{F}_p G$;
(b) $\mathcal{N}(G;p)$ comes equipped with a dimension function $\dim_{\mathcal{N}(G;p)}$ on finitely generated projective $\mathcal{N}(G;p)$-modules which extends to all $\mathcal{N}(G;p)$-modules in a way sufficient to define characteristic-$p$ $L^2$-Betti numbers $b_n^{(2)}(G;\mathbb{F}_p) := \dim_{\mathcal{N}(G;p)} H_n(G;\mathcal{N}(G;p))$;
(c) for every residually finite $G$ and every normal chain $(G_i)_{i\ge 0}$ of finite-index normal subgroups of $G$ with $\bigcap_i G_i = 1$, the normalized Betti numbers $\dim_{\mathbb{F}_p} H_n(G_i;\mathbb{F}_p)/[G:G_i]$ converge as $i\to\infty$ to $b_n^{(2)}(G;\mathbb{F}_p)$?

Consider soft amorphous materials (such as colloidal gels, glasses, emulsions, foams, and dense suspensions) subjected to simple shear. A "shear band" is a spatially localized region where the strain rate differs significantly from the rest of the sample, and shear banding is called "long-lasting" (or steady-state) if such localization persists over experimental/simulation timescales rather than being a transient on the way to homogeneous flow. Microscopic control parameters of interest include:
(i) the strength and range of attractive interactions between constituent particles;
(ii) the sample age, defined as the time elapsed since the most recent rejuvenation (e.g., a strong pre-shear that erases prior history);
(iii) the geometric shape of the particles (e.g., spherical vs. anisotropic);
(iv) the surface roughness of the particles;
(v) the presence of overconstrained microscopic domains—local regions whose number of mechanical constraints exceeds the isostatic threshold—that are trapped in the bulk upon sample preparation and that may reorganize structurally under shear.

Address the following questions at the microscopic level:
(a) Is some nonzero degree of interparticle attraction necessary for the existence of long-lasting (steady-state) shear bands, or can purely repulsive systems also exhibit persistent shear banding?
(b) Provide a quantitative microscopic theory describing how sample age, particle shape, and particle surface roughness control the emergence, persistence, and possible delayed onset of shear bands.
(c) Characterize how overconstrained microscopic domains prepared in the bulk, and their structural reorganization under shear, control the appearance and lifetime of shear bands, including the duration of long-lasting transients before homogeneous flow is restored.

Consider a large pretrained autoregressive language model T (the "teacher") with parameter count N_T, trained on a broad text corpus, and a target downstream task distribution D (for example, a specific classification, summarization, or instruction-following task) on which T attains performance P(T, D) under a fixed evaluation metric. Develop a principled methodology for constructing a substantially smaller language model S (the "student"), with parameter count N_S satisfying N_S ≪ N_T, such that:

(a) S achieves performance P(S, D) on the target task within a prescribed tolerance ε of P(T, D);
(b) S satisfies prescribed inference-cost constraints (latency, memory, energy) that T does not satisfy;
(c) the training procedure used to produce S from T is itself resource-efficient.

In particular, characterize the optimal combination and theoretical role of the following techniques in producing S:
  (i) parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT), in which only a small subset or low-rank adaptation of the parameters of a base model is updated;
  (ii) teacher–student knowledge distillation, in which S is trained to match the output distributions, hidden representations, or logits of T on a chosen data distribution;
  (iii) other forms of model compression and distillation, including sequence-level distillation, distribution matching on synthetic data generated by T, pruning, and quantization.

The problem is to determine, for given (N_T, N_S, D, ε) and resource budget, an optimal training scheme combining (i)–(iii) and to provide quantitative guarantees on the resulting performance gap P(T, D) − P(S, D), the sample complexity, and the compute required.

Consider the standard Hilbert-space formalism of non-relativistic quantum mechanics: pure states are represented by unit vectors (rays) in a complex separable Hilbert space H, observables by self-adjoint operators on H, the Born rule assigns probabilities |⟨ψ|φ⟩|² to measurement outcomes, and composite systems are described on tensor products H_A ⊗ H_B. In this framework, consider the Einstein–Podolsky–Rosen scenario: two systems A and B are prepared in an entangled state on H_A ⊗ H_B such that measurements of suitably chosen observables on A and on B exhibit perfect correlations and the corresponding observables on B have eigenvalues that can be predicted with certainty conditional on the outcome of a measurement on A, while the choice of which observable to measure on A is free.

Provide a coherent interpretation of this quantum formalism that:
(a) is consistent with all empirical predictions of quantum mechanics, including the perfect EPR correlations;
(b) does not assume that the underlying dynamics or measurement outcomes are deterministic, i.e., does not require hidden variables that fix every measurement outcome in advance;
(c) clarifies the physical meaning of the state vector ψ (in particular whether it represents an individual system or only a statistical ensemble of similarly prepared systems), the role of probability, and the status of non-commuting observables;
(d) explains the apparent non-locality of the EPR correlations without contradicting relativistic causality.

State precisely the principles of such an interpretation and demonstrate, by argument from the formalism, that it satisfies (a)–(d).

Let a "theory" T be a set of statements in some formal language, and let C(T) denote its content, defined as the set of (non-tautological) statements logically entailed by T. Define the logical probability p(T) of T (relative to a fixed probability measure on the space of statements) so that, ceteris paribus, a theory forbidding more possibilities has lower probability. Define the testability of T to be a measure that increases as C(T) increases (equivalently, as the class of potential falsifiers of T grows), and call a test of T "severe" to the degree that it has high prior probability of refuting T if T is false. Finally, give a precise explication of the "simplicity" S(T) of a theory in terms of the structure of its potential falsifiers (for instance, the minimum number of parameters or the dimension of the family of possible counterexamples needed to falsify T).

Provide such definitions of content, logical probability, testability, severity, and simplicity, and then prove the following relationship: for theories T comparable under this notion of simplicity,
(i) S(T) is monotonically related to the logical improbability 1 − p(T) of T, not to p(T);
(ii) S(T) is monotonically related to the testability of T;
(iii) consequently, choosing the simplest theory first among empirically adequate competitors maximizes the prior chance of subjecting the chosen theory to severe tests.

Formulate the framework precisely and establish (i)–(iii), or else show that no such framework simultaneously satisfies (i)–(iii) under reasonable axioms.

For a positive integer $N$, work in the cyclic group $\mathbb{Z}/N\mathbb{Z}$. For a subset $A\subseteq\mathbb{Z}/N\mathbb{Z}$, let $1_A$ denote its indicator function and define its Fourier coefficients by $\widehat{1_A}(r)=\tfrac{1}{N}\sum_{x\in\mathbb{Z}/N\mathbb{Z}} 1_A(x)e^{-2\pi i rx/N}$ for $r\in\mathbb{Z}/N\mathbb{Z}$. Say that $A$ is $\alpha$-uniform if $\max_{r\neq 0}|\widehat{1_A}(r)|\le\alpha$. The number of four-term arithmetic progressions in $A$ is $\#\{(x,d)\in(\mathbb{Z}/N\mathbb{Z})^2:x,x+d,x+2d,x+3d\in A\}$.

Question: Is it true that for every fixed integer $k\ge 1$ and every sufficiently small $c>0$ (depending on $k$), there exist a sequence of positive integers $N_1<N_2<\cdots$, a sequence of subsets $A_i\subseteq\mathbb{Z}/N_i\mathbb{Z}$, and a sequence of real numbers $\alpha_i>0$ with $\lim_{i\to\infty}\alpha_i=0$, such that for every $i\ge 1$:
(i) $|A_i|\ge c N_i$;
(ii) $A_i$ is $\alpha_i$-uniform;
(iii) $A_i$ contains at most $c^k N_i^2$ four-term arithmetic progressions?

Consider the proposed construct of "team psychological safety," defined as a shared belief held by members of a team that the team is safe for interpersonal risk taking (e.g., speaking up with ideas, questions, concerns, or mistakes without fear of negative interpersonal consequences). The construct is hypothesized to be distinct from related team-level constructs such as group cohesiveness, interpersonal trust, work group satisfaction, and team efficacy.

Address the following:

1. Provide a theoretical specification of team psychological safety that distinguishes it conceptually from each of the related constructs above (especially group cohesiveness, interpersonal trust at the dyadic level, and team efficacy), including the level of analysis (team-level shared belief vs. individual or dyadic), the referent (interpersonal risk-taking climate vs. attraction, reliance, or capability beliefs), and the time horizon.

2. Develop and validate a measurement instrument (e.g., a multi-item survey scale aggregated to the team level with justified aggregation statistics such as within-team agreement r_WG and intraclass correlation coefficients ICC(1) and ICC(2)) that demonstrates:
   (a) internal consistency (e.g., Cronbach's α) and test–retest reliability;
   (b) convergent validity with theoretically related but distinct constructs;
   (c) discriminant validity from group cohesiveness, interpersonal trust, work group satisfaction, and team efficacy, established through confirmatory factor analysis in which a multi-factor model fits significantly better than alternative models that merge psychological safety with each related construct;
   (d) nomological/predictive validity through demonstrated associations with downstream outcomes (e.g., learning behavior, error reporting, team performance) above and beyond the related constructs.

3. Determine, on the basis of (1) and (2), whether team psychological safety is an empirically distinct construct from each of the listed related constructs, and identify what additional conceptual and empirical work is required to refine the construct's meaning, boundary conditions, antecedents, and consequences.

Let $S$ be an orientable surface of finite type (say of genus $g$ with $n$ punctures, with $3g-3+n\ge 1$), and let $\mathcal{T}(S)$ denote its Teichmüller space, with $\mathrm{Mod}(S)$ the mapping class group of $S$ acting on $\mathcal{T}(S)$ by the natural action.

A compactification of $\mathcal{T}(S)$ is a compact Hausdorff space $\overline{\mathcal{T}}(S)$ containing $\mathcal{T}(S)$ as an open dense subset. Such a compactification is said to be:
\begin{itemize}
\item \emph{contractible} if $\overline{\mathcal{T}}(S)$ is contractible (and one typically also requires it to be a Euclidean retract / locally contractible, so that the boundary $Z = \overline{\mathcal{T}}(S)\setminus \mathcal{T}(S)$ is a $Z$-set);
\item \emph{small at infinity} (in the sense of Carlsson–Pedersen) if for every compact subset $K \subset \mathcal{T}(S)$ and every point $z\in Z$, and every neighborhood $U$ of $z$ in $\overline{\mathcal{T}}(S)$, there is a smaller neighborhood $V \subset U$ of $z$ such that any translate $gK$ with $g\in\mathrm{Mod}(S)$ that meets $V$ is contained in $U$;
\item \emph{equivariant} (with respect to $\mathrm{Mod}(S)$) if the action of $\mathrm{Mod}(S)$ on $\mathcal{T}(S)$ extends to a continuous action on $\overline{\mathcal{T}}(S)$.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Question.} Does there exist a compactification $\overline{\mathcal{T}}(S)$ of Teichmüller space which is simultaneously contractible, small at infinity, and equivariant with respect to the action of the mapping class group $\mathrm{Mod}(S)$?

(Such a compactification, also called an $\mathcal{E}\mathcal{Z}$-structure for $\mathrm{Mod}(S)$, would imply injectivity of the assembly map in the Farrell–Jones / Baum–Connes type isomorphism conjectures for $\mathrm{Mod}(S)$.)

Let $\Gamma$ be a discrete group. A family $\mathcal{F}$ of subgroups of $\Gamma$ is a non-empty collection of subgroups closed under conjugation and under taking subgroups. Write $\mathcal{FIN}$ for the family of finite subgroups of $\Gamma$, and $\mathcal{VC}$ for the family of virtually cyclic subgroups of $\Gamma$. For a family $\mathcal{F}$, let $E_{\mathcal F}\Gamma$ denote a classifying space for $\Gamma$-actions with isotropy in $\mathcal{F}$, i.e., a $\Gamma$-CW-complex whose $H$-fixed-point set is contractible for $H\in\mathcal F$ and empty otherwise; let $\underline{\mathrm{gd}}_{\mathcal F}\Gamma$ denote its minimal $\Gamma$-CW dimension.

Say that a family $\mathcal{F}$ with $\mathcal{FIN}\subseteq\mathcal{F}\subseteq\mathcal{VC}$ is "adapted to the pair $\mathcal{FIN}\subset\mathcal{VC}$" if it arises naturally from the geometric/algebraic structure of $\Gamma$ (for instance, from a preferred collection of infinite cyclic or virtually cyclic subgroups) and the inclusions $\mathcal{FIN}\subseteq\mathcal{F}\subseteq\mathcal{VC}$ are both strict whenever possible.

Problem. For each of the following classical families of finitely generated groups, identify a non-trivial collection $\mathcal{F}$ of subgroups adapted to $\mathcal{FIN}\subset\mathcal{VC}$:
(a) word-hyperbolic groups;
(b) CAT(0) groups;
(c) mapping class groups of surfaces;
(d) outer automorphism groups $\mathrm{Out}(F_n)$ of free groups;
(e) arithmetic groups, lattices in semisimple Lie groups;
(f) Coxeter and Artin groups.

Furthermore, given such an adapted family $\mathcal{F}$, determine whether one can construct a model of $E_{\mathcal F}\Gamma$ whose dimension is smaller than $\underline{\mathrm{gd}}_{\mathcal{VC}}\Gamma$, and compare it with $\underline{\mathrm{gd}}_{\mathcal{FIN}}\Gamma$.

Within the framework of modern cosmology (a spatially homogeneous and isotropic Friedmann–Lemaître–Robertson–Walker spacetime with scale factor a(t) governed by the Friedmann equations sourced by matter, radiation, and a dark-energy component with equation of state p = w·ρ), determine:

(a) A physically consistent account of how the initial conditions permitting the existence of a universe (a finite-energy, low-entropy initial state with the observed values of fundamental constants and matter content) came to be — i.e., identify and justify the mechanism responsible for the origin of these conditions.

(b) The ultimate long-time asymptotic behavior of a(t) and of the cosmological fluid, deciding among the following scenarios:
   (i) Big Freeze: a(t) → ∞ monotonically with all matter cooling toward thermodynamic equilibrium at zero temperature;
   (ii) Big Rip: there exists a finite time t* < ∞ such that a(t) → ∞ as t → t*, driven by phantom dark energy with w < −1, leading to the disintegration of all bound structures;
   (iii) Big Crunch: a(t) attains a maximum and subsequently decreases to 0 in finite time, recollapsing the universe;
   (iv) Big Bounce: a(t) attains a positive minimum a_min > 0 and rebounds, so that the present expansion is preceded by a contracting phase.

Provide the determination of which scenario is realized in our universe, together with the requisite cosmological observations or theoretical principles that select it.

Model the spatial part of the universe at a fixed cosmic time as a connected, complete, orientable Riemannian 3-manifold $(M,g)$ of constant sectional curvature $k$ (a comoving spatial section of a Friedmann–Lemaître–Robertson–Walker spacetime). Observational evidence constrains $k$ to be close to $0$ on the scale of the observable universe, but neither the sign of $k$ nor the global topology of $M$ is determined by local geometry.

Determine the 3-manifold $M$ underlying comoving space. In particular, decide which of the following holds:
(a) $M$ is unmeasurable in the sense that no cosmological observation can in principle distinguish its global topology from alternatives;
(b) $M$ is the Poincaré dodecahedral space (the spherical 3-manifold $S^{3}/I^{*}$, where $I^{*}$ is the binary icosahedral group acting freely on $S^{3}$ by isometries);
(c) $M$ is some other 3-manifold, in which case identify its homeomorphism type (for example, a Euclidean space form such as $\mathbb{R}^{3}$ or a 3-torus $\mathbb{R}^{3}/\Lambda$, a spherical space form $S^{3}/\Gamma$, or a hyperbolic 3-manifold $\mathbb{H}^{3}/\Gamma$).

Nuclear fission reactors produce a flux of electron antineutrinos $\bar\nu_e$ from the beta decays of fission fragments of the principal fissile isotopes ${}^{235}\mathrm{U}$, ${}^{238}\mathrm{U}$, ${}^{239}\mathrm{Pu}$, and ${}^{241}\mathrm{Pu}$. Standard summation and conversion calculations (e.g., the Huber–Mueller model) predict an absolute $\bar\nu_e$ emission rate and energy spectrum per fission. Short-baseline experiments that detect reactor antineutrinos via the inverse beta-decay reaction $\bar\nu_e + p \to e^+ + n$ consistently measure an integrated flux that is approximately $94\%$ of the predicted value, a roughly $6\%$ deficit known as the reactor antineutrino anomaly.

Determine the origin of this deficit. Specifically, decide among the following (mutually non-exclusive) possibilities and quantify the contribution of each:
(i) new physics beyond the Standard Model, in particular oscillations of $\bar\nu_e$ into one or more light sterile neutrino states $\nu_s$ with mass-squared splitting $\Delta m^2 \sim 1\,\mathrm{eV}^2$ and mixing parameter $\sin^2 2\theta$ to be determined;
(ii) systematic experimental errors in the absolute normalization of the short-baseline measurements;
(iii) errors in the theoretical reactor antineutrino flux calculations, in particular in the modeling of the beta-decay branches of fission fragments of ${}^{235}\mathrm{U}$ (and possibly other isotopes).

Provide a quantitative explanation of the observed $\sim 6\%$ deficit and of the energy-dependent spectral distortion (the "5 MeV bump") seen in the measured spectrum.

The cosmological principle asserts that on sufficiently large scales the spatial distribution of matter and radiation in the universe is statistically homogeneous and isotropic, so that the spacetime geometry is well-modeled by a Friedmann–Lemaître–Robertson–Walker (FLRW) metric. Under this assumption, any observed dipole anisotropy in the number counts and flux of distant extragalactic sources should arise solely from the Solar System's peculiar motion relative to the cosmic rest frame, and should agree in amplitude and direction with the kinematic dipole inferred from the cosmic microwave background (CMB).

Consider the following empirical inputs:
(a) From the NRAO VLA Sky Survey (NVSS) radio catalogue, measurements of the dipole in source number counts, in total intensity, in polarization density, and in degree of polarization, all pointing in a consistent direction;
(b) The CMB kinematic dipole derived from assuming that the CMB temperature dipole is purely due to the local barycentric motion;
(c) Observations of large-scale alignment of optical polarization vectors of quasars over scales on the order of a gigaparsec;
(d) Reported anomalous features (alignments, hemispherical asymmetry, low-quadrupole/quadrupole-octupole alignment) in the angular power spectrum and statistical isotropy tests of the CMB temperature map.

Problem: Determine whether the universe, viewed on its largest observable scales, is statistically isotropic in the sense required by the cosmological principle. Concretely, decide whether the discrepancy between the radio (NVSS-type) source dipole and the CMB kinematic dipole, the gigaparsec-scale alignment of quasar optical polarizations, and the reported large-angle CMB anomalies can be reconciled with a statistically isotropic FLRW cosmology (allowing for known systematic effects, selection biases, and standard ΛCDM stochastic fluctuations), or whether they instead require an intrinsic large-scale anisotropy invalidating the cosmological principle. Provide either a model within statistically isotropic ΛCDM that quantitatively accounts for all of (a)–(d) at acceptable statistical significance, or a demonstration that no such model exists and an explicit anisotropic cosmological model consistent with the data.

In the framework of quantum chromodynamics (QCD) describing the strong interactions of quarks and gluons, consider the high-energy (small Bjorken-x) regime of hadronic and nuclear collisions in which the gluon density inside hadrons grows rapidly. Address the following interconnected questions about the formation of a quark–gluon plasma:

1. Saturation. As the gluon occupation number becomes large at small momentum fraction x, do nonlinear effects in the QCD evolution cause the gluon distribution to saturate, i.e. cease to grow without bound, and instead approach a state characterized by a dynamically generated saturation scale Q_s(x)?

2. Colour Glass Condensate. Do the saturated gluons in this regime form a coherent, dense, weakly coupled but highly populated state of matter — the so-called Colour Glass Condensate — and what are its precise theoretical properties (energy–momentum tensor, correlators of color fields, initial conditions for thermalization) and experimentally measurable signatures?

3. Evolution equations. For the following high-energy QCD evolution equations:
   (a) the Balitsky–Fadin–Kuraev–Lipatov (BFKL) equation, which resums leading logarithms of 1/x in the linear regime;
   (b) the Balitsky–Kovchegov (BK) equation, the nonlinear extension of BFKL in the large-N_c limit incorporating saturation;
   (c) the Catani–Ciafaloni–Fiorani–Marchesini (CCFM) equation, which interpolates between DGLAP and BFKL via angular ordering;

identify the distinctive theoretical signatures (e.g. predicted small-x behavior of structure functions F_2 and F_L, diffractive cross sections, forward particle production, geometric scaling) and the experimental evidence from deep inelastic scattering and hadronic/nuclear collisions that selects among or supports these evolution dynamics.

Provide a quantitative theoretical description and identify decisive experimental observables that establish (or refute) gluon saturation, the existence of a Colour Glass Condensate, and the validity of BFKL, BK, and CCFM evolution.

Design and carry out an experimental test of a Bell inequality that simultaneously closes the principal experimental loopholes — in particular the locality (communication) loophole, the detection (fair-sampling) loophole, and the freedom-of-choice loophole — and which yields a statistically significant violation of a Bell-type inequality (e.g., the CHSH inequality), thereby refuting the class of local hidden-variable theories.

Concretely: consider two spatially separated measurement stations A and B, each receiving one particle of an entangled pair. At each run, an independent random number generator at each station selects a measurement setting (a ∈ {a₁, a₂} at A; b ∈ {b₁, b₂} at B), producing binary outcomes A(a), B(b) ∈ {−1, +1}. Let
  S = E(a₁,b₁) + E(a₁,b₂) + E(a₂,b₁) − E(a₂,b₂),
where E(a,b) denotes the correlation of outcomes. Any local hidden-variable model satisfies |S| ≤ 2. The task is to produce an experimental realization in which:
 (i) the choice of setting at each station and the registration of the outcome at the other station are space-like separated (locality loophole closed);
 (ii) all entangled pairs prepared are accounted for in the statistics so that no fair-sampling assumption is required (detection loophole closed);
 (iii) the setting choices are made by independent, suitably random processes (freedom-of-choice loophole closed);
and in which the measured value of S violates the bound |S| ≤ 2 with high statistical significance.

Gamma-ray bursts (GRBs) are intense, short-lived flashes of gamma radiation observed from cosmological distances. They are empirically divided into two duration classes by the parameter T90 (the time interval during which 90% of the burst's fluence is emitted): "short" GRBs with T90 < 2 s, and "long" GRBs with T90 > 2 s. The standard "collapsar" model holds that long-duration GRBs originate from the gravitational collapse of the rapidly rotating core of a massive star, producing a relativistic jet and an accompanying broad-lined Type Ic supernova. Several long-duration events (notably GRB 060614, detected by the Swift satellite, with T90 ≈ 102 s) show deep observational limits ruling out any associated bright supernova at the burst's host-galaxy distance, contradicting the standard collapsar picture.

Problem: Provide a physically self-consistent classification and progenitor model for long-duration gamma-ray bursts that:
(a) reproduces the well-established association between most long-duration GRBs and Type Ic broad-lined supernovae via the collapsar mechanism;
(b) simultaneously accounts for the existence of long-duration GRBs (such as GRB 060614-like events) for which deep follow-up observations exclude any accompanying supernova above stringent luminosity limits;
(c) predicts observable distinguishing signatures (e.g., host-galaxy properties, spectral-temporal characteristics such as spectral lag and peak energy, kilonova-like afterglow components, or environmental offsets) that allow unambiguous classification of an individual long-duration burst between competing progenitor channels (e.g., failed-supernova collapsars versus compact-object mergers with extended emission).

Consider a classical mechanical system whose phase-space dynamics is driven by external nonconservative forces and is simultaneously subjected to deterministic thermostatting forces that, on average, balance the work done by the driving forces, so that the system settles into a stationary (generally far-from-equilibrium) probability distribution on phase space.

Provide a fundamental definition of the entropy creation (production) rate for such a system that satisfies all of the following requirements:

(i) it applies to very general classical systems in stationary states, without assuming the state is close to equilibrium;
(ii) it does not presuppose the existence of a well-defined temperature, so it must remain meaningful in regimes where temperature itself need not admit a proper definition;
(iii) it reduces, in the appropriate equilibrium or near-equilibrium limit, to the standard notion of entropy production;
(iv) it is operationally accessible, in the sense that it can in principle be measured in numerical simulations and in laboratory experiments;
(v) it leads to a coherent theory of "nonequilibrium ensembles" yielding nontrivial, testable predictions.

Either give such a definition together with a proof of its general applicability, or prove that no definition satisfying (i)–(v) can exist.

Propose a uniform, stakeholder-neutral definition of "engineering/design deliverable quality" for capital construction projects, together with an associated quantitative measurement methodology. The definition and measurement method should satisfy the following requirements:

1. Applicability: It must be meaningful and usable by all of the following classes of project stakeholders: project owners, constructors and craft labor, equipment suppliers, and engineers/designers.

2. Definition: Provide a precise specification of what constitutes an "engineering/design deliverable" (e.g., drawings, specifications, models, calculations, datasheets) and what aspects of such a deliverable count toward its "quality" (e.g., accuracy, completeness, clarity, timeliness, constructability, consistency across disciplines, compliance with standards).

3. Measurement method: Define a procedure that assigns to any given engineering/design deliverable D a quality score Q(D) (which may be scalar- or vector-valued) such that:
   (a) the inputs to the procedure are observable or auditable attributes of D;
   (b) the procedure is reproducible, in the sense that two independent evaluators applying it to the same D obtain scores within a stated tolerance;
   (c) the scoring scale is calibrated so that scores are comparable across deliverables of different types and across different projects;
   (d) the method admits aggregation rules to produce package-level or project-level quality measures from individual deliverable scores.

4. Validation criteria: State the criteria under which the proposed definition and measurement method would be considered validated (for example, statistical correlation of Q(D) with downstream rework, change orders, schedule slippage, or cost overruns).

The task is to construct such a definition Q, the associated measurement methodology, and the validation framework, and to demonstrate that the construction meets requirements (1)–(4).

A mega-project is a large-scale, capital-intensive undertaking (typically costing in excess of one billion US dollars) involving substantial complexity, long planning horizons, many stakeholders, and significant social, environmental, and economic impact. Empirical studies of such projects consistently report systematic overruns in cost and schedule, as well as frequent shortfalls in delivered benefits relative to ex-ante forecasts.

Formulate and justify a set of changes to the processes of project development (including front-end planning, scoping, cost and benefit estimation, risk assessment, stakeholder engagement, governance, and contracting) and project execution (including procurement, scheduling, supply-chain coordination, control, monitoring, and decision-making under uncertainty) that, taken together, would demonstrably increase the probability that a mega-project is delivered on budget, on schedule, and with its promised performance and benefits. The proposed framework should:

(1) identify which structural, behavioral, and informational factors are the dominant drivers of failure in current practice;
(2) specify concrete modifications to development and execution practices that address those drivers;
(3) provide a quantitative or otherwise rigorous argument (e.g., via statistical analysis of historical project outcomes, decision-theoretic modeling, optimization under uncertainty, or systems-dynamics simulation) showing that the proposed changes lead to improved outcomes;
(4) discuss the conditions under which the prescription is robust across different project types (e.g., infrastructure, energy, defense, IT) and institutional contexts.

For a real number $x$, let $\|x\|$ denote the distance from $x$ to the nearest integer, i.e. $\|x\| = \min_{m \in \mathbb{Z}} |x - m|$. Does there exist an absolute constant $c > 0$ such that
$$\|\log n\| > n^{-c}$$
for every integer $n \ge 2$? (Here $\log$ denotes the natural logarithm.)

Let $p \geq 7$ be a prime number. Determine all triples of integers $(x, y, z) \in \mathbb{Z}^3$ satisfying the following three conditions simultaneously:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item $xyz \neq 0$;
  \item $\gcd(x, y, z) = 1$;
  \item $x^p + 2 y^p = z^2$.
\end{enumerate}
That is, classify all primitive nontrivial integer solutions of the generalized Fermat-type equation $x^p + 2 y^p = z^2$ for every prime exponent $p \geq 7$.

A partizan combinatorial game is a finite two-player game between Left and Right with perfect information and no chance, in which the legal moves available to Left and to Right from any position may differ. Each position G has a set of Left options G^L and Right options G^R, and play proceeds with the players alternating in some order; under the normal-play convention, the player unable to move loses. The disjunctive sum G + H is the game in which a player on their turn chooses one of the component games G or H and makes a legal move in that component.

Say that a partizan game G "has a polynomial-time strategy" if there is an algorithm running in time polynomial in the size of the encoding of the current position that, given any position reachable in G and the identity of the player to move, decides whether that player has a winning strategy (equivalently, outputs an optimal move).

Decide whether there exist three partizan games G_1, G_2, G_3 with the following properties:
(i) each of G_1, G_2, G_3 individually has a polynomial-time strategy;
(ii) each of the adjacent disjunctive sums G_1 + G_2 and G_2 + G_3 has a polynomial-time strategy;
(iii) every option of G_1, G_2, G_3 (and recursively, all positions reachable in these games and in the sums in (ii)) admits a polynomial-time strategy;
(iv) nevertheless, determining the winner of the disjunctive sum G_1 + G_3 is NP-hard.

Either exhibit such a triple (G_1, G_2, G_3), or prove that no such triple exists.

In combinatorial game theory, every short partizan game G has a unique simplest representative under the equivalence G = H iff G + (-H) is a second-player win; this representative is called the canonical form of G, and a canonical-form engine is an algorithm that, given a game tree, computes such canonical forms (eliminating dominated options and bypassing reversible moves) and supports operations such as disjunctive sum, negation, comparison with numbers/switches/stars, and evaluation of temperature and atomic weight. Separately, classical game-tree search algorithms (e.g., alpha-beta search, proof-number search, transposition tables, Monte Carlo tree search) explore positions of a specific ruleset to determine win/loss values for concrete positions.

Design and rigorously analyze a hybrid algorithm that integrates standard game-tree search methods with a canonical-form engine for short partizan combinatorial games, in such a way that:
(i) the algorithm decomposes positions into disjunctive sums of components whenever possible and replaces components by their canonical forms;
(ii) the search procedure uses values, temperatures, and atomic weights computed by the canonical-form engine as heuristics and pruning bounds (in place of, or alongside, conventional evaluation functions);
(iii) the canonical-form engine, in turn, uses search to certify equalities, inequalities, or simplifications of game values that are too costly to verify by exhaustive enumeration over all options;
(iv) the integrated system is provably correct (returns the exact game-theoretic value or outcome class of any input position from a fixed ruleset, given sufficient resources) and is demonstrably more efficient, on a well-defined class of positions, than either pure search or pure canonical-form computation in isolation.

Specify the data structures, the interface between the two components, the soundness and completeness proofs, and a complexity or empirical performance analysis on a benchmark ruleset (for example, Domineering, Amazons endgames, or Konane).

A combinatorial game is a two-player perfect-information game with no chance moves, in which the two players (Left and Right) alternate moves; a position is specified by the sets of options available to each player, and play ends when the player to move has no legal option. Under the normal convention, the player unable to move loses; under the misère convention, that player wins. Given two component games G and H, several operations combine them into a single compound game:

(i) the disjunctive sum G + H, in which a player on their turn chooses one component and moves in it;
(ii) the conjunctive compound, in which a player must move in every component simultaneously;
(iii) the selective (or shortened) compound, in which a player moves in any nonempty subset of components;
(iv) the nearly disjunctive compound, intermediate between disjunctive and selective play, in which the rules constrain how often or under what conditions a player may move in more than one component;
(v) the WHIM-type join, in which it is an option for the player to move to switch which subgame is currently active.

Problem. Develop a unified theory of "joins" of combinatorial games that:

(a) gives a precise definition of when two component games G and H are "compatible" under a prescribed join operation, so that the value (or outcome class) of the compound is determined by invariants of G and H individually;
(b) provides, for each of the normal and misère conventions, a calculus (analogous to the Sprague–Grundy theory for disjunctive sums under normal play) that computes the outcome or value of the compound from data attached to the components, including the case of nearly disjunctive compositions;
(c) characterizes the joins under which a player is allowed to switch between component games (as in WHIM) and determines the relationship between such switching joins and the standard disjunctive, conjunctive, selective, and nearly disjunctive joins;
(d) describes how to translate, or switch, between the normal-play theory and the misère-play theory of such joins, identifying the algebraic structures (monoids, quotients, or partial orders of games) that govern each.

For an integer $d \geq 2$, a convex polytope $P \subset \mathbb{R}^d$ is said to admit a monohedral tiling of $\mathbb{R}^d$ if there exists a family $\{P_i\}_{i\in I}$ of subsets of $\mathbb{R}^d$ such that:
(i) each $P_i$ is congruent to $P$ (i.e. obtained from $P$ by an isometry of $\mathbb{R}^d$);
(ii) $\bigcup_{i\in I} P_i = \mathbb{R}^d$;
(iii) the interiors of distinct $P_i$ and $P_j$ are disjoint.

Give a complete classification, up to similarity, of all convex polytopes $P \subset \mathbb{R}^d$ that admit a monohedral tiling of $\mathbb{R}^d$. In particular, solve this classification problem in every dimension $d \geq 2$, including the planar case $d=2$ (where $P$ is a convex polygon).

Does there exist an absolute constant c > 0 with the following property? For every positive integer k there exists n_0 = n_0(k) such that for every n ≥ n_0 and every partition {1, 2, …, n} = A_1 ∪ A_2 ∪ … ∪ A_k of the first n positive integers into k (disjoint) classes, the set
   S := { m ≤ n : m = a + a' for some j ∈ {1,…,k} and some a, a' ∈ A_j }
of integers at most n that can be written as a sum of two (not necessarily distinct) elements lying in a common class satisfies |S| > c·n.

Let $\mathcal{E}$ be an elementary topos. Call a left exact endofunctor $F: \mathcal{E} \to \mathcal{E}$ coherently idempotent if it is equipped with a coherent idempotent 2-cell exhibiting $F \circ F \simeq F$ in the 2-category $\mathbf{Lex}$ of toposes and left exact functors. By a theorem of Paré, Rosebrugh, and Wood, every such coherently idempotent left exact endofunctor splits in $\mathbf{Lex}$ into two steps: one in which the inclusion of the splitting object is left adjoint to the projection (a "quotient" of $\mathcal{E}$), and one in which it is right adjoint (a "subtopos" inclusion). In this way the coherently idempotent left exact endofunctors parameterize the subquotients of $\mathcal{E}$. The collection $L(\mathcal{E})$ of (equivalence classes of) such subquotients carries a natural lattice structure inherited from the corresponding left exact subcategories.

Define two operators on $L(\mathcal{E})$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $c_s: L(\mathcal{E}) \to L(\mathcal{E})$ sends a subquotient to the smallest subtopos of $\mathcal{E}$ containing its inverse image;
\item $c_q: L(\mathcal{E}) \to L(\mathcal{E})$ sends a subtopos to the smallest quotient of $\mathcal{E}$ for which the composite with the inclusion is again an inclusion, extended to subquotients in the analogous way.
\end{itemize}
Each $c_s, c_q$ is a closure operator on $L(\mathcal{E})$, and they retract onto the sublattices of subtoposes and of quotients of $\mathcal{E}$, respectively.

Determine the algebraic properties of the lattice $L(\mathcal{E})$ equipped with the pair of closure operators $(c_s, c_q)$: identify the equational and order-theoretic axioms (e.g., distributivity, modularity, interaction laws between $c_s$ and $c_q$, fixed-point characterizations of the two retract sublattices) governing this structure, and give an abstract axiomatization of such "doubly-closed" lattices that arise from the subquotients of an elementary topos.

Construct a formal axiomatic theory of probability with the following properties. The primitive objects are elements a, b, c, ... of an abstract set S, together with a binary operation (a, b) ↦ ab on S (intended to represent conjunction/intersection), and a real-valued two-place function p(a, b) (read as the probability of a given b), defined for all a, b in S. The axioms must:

1. Imply the standard Kolmogorov-style laws of probability when a suitable absolute probability p(a) is defined (for example by p(a) = p(a, e) for a designated element e representing tautological/zero evidence, or by using a limiting/normalization condition), including:
   (i) 0 ≤ p(a, b) ≤ 1 for all a, b,
   (ii) p(a, a) = 1,
   (iii) a multiplication law p(ab, c) = p(a, bc) · p(b, c),
   (iv) an addition law for "complementary" or "disjoint" elements, derivable from the axioms without assuming the existence of a complement a priori.

2. Be neutral between interpretations, in the sense that the same axiom system admits at least the following models:
   (a) S = statements of a formal language, p(a, b) = the logical probability (degree of confirmation/credibility) of statement a given evidence b, with absolute logical probability p(a) corresponding to b = tautology;
   (b) S = events in a sample space or members of a "collective"/ensemble, p(a, b) = relative frequency of a within b;
   (c) S = contents (sets of logical consequences of statements), or deductive systems, with p interpreted as a measure on contents;
   (d) S = a Boolean algebra of classes, with p a normalized measure;
   (e) S = propositions of a propositional calculus, with p a normalized valuation;
   (f) S = physical set-ups or experimental arrangements, with p(a, b) interpreted as the propensity of outcome a in conditions b.

Provide an explicit, finite, autonomous (i.e., not presupposing measure theory, Boolean algebra, or any particular interpretation in its statement) set of axioms on the triple (S, ·, p) from which the standard calculus of probability follows and which is satisfied by each of the models (a)–(f) above.

Provide a precise account of "scientific objectivity" that respects the following distinction without collapsing it:

(a) the descriptive (quid facti) problem: giving a faithful description and classification of the inductive policies, procedures, or reasoning patterns that human inquirers in fact employ, including an assessment of which such procedures are more or less successful or reliable in practice;

(b) the normative or justificatory (quid juris) problem: determining whether, and under what conditions, inductive arguments — i.e., inferences from observed instances to general conclusions or to unobserved instances — are rationally warranted.

The task is to formulate a notion of scientific objectivity, together with explicit criteria distinguishing objective from non-objective scientific claims or procedures, such that:
  (i) the account does not reduce (b) to (a), i.e., it does not treat the question of justification as answered merely by a successful descriptive or sociological classification of actual inductive practice;
  (ii) the account does not reduce (a) to (b);
  (iii) the account explains how scientific objectivity can be sustained given that any descriptive account of inductive practice, by itself, leaves the justificatory question (b) open.

State the proposed criterion of objectivity, the argument that it satisfies (i)–(iii), and the consequences for the role (if any) of induction in empirical science.

For any class $X$ of groups, define the following operators producing new classes of groups:
- $H(X)$ is the class of all homomorphic images of groups in $X$;
- $S(X)$ is the class of all groups isomorphic to a subgroup of some group in $X$;
- $P(X)$ is the class of all groups isomorphic to an unrestricted direct product $\prod_{i\in I} G_i$ of an arbitrary family $(G_i)_{i\in I}$ of groups in $X$;
- $P_f(X)$ is the class of all groups isomorphic to a direct product of a finite family of groups in $X$.

By Birkhoff's HSP theorem, $H(S(P(X)))$ is the variety of groups generated by $X$.

A group is called metabelian if it has an abelian normal subgroup with abelian quotient (equivalently, its commutator subgroup is abelian).

Question: Is it true that for every class $M$ consisting of metabelian groups,
$$H(S(P_f(M))) \subseteq S(H(P(S(M))))\,?$$

Work within the framework of groups of finite Morley rank (i.e., groups equipped with a ranked first-order structure in the sense of model theory, where every interpretable set has a well-defined finite ordinal rank). A group is called connected if it has no proper definable subgroup of finite index. An action of a group G on a group H is said to be:
- definable, if the action map G × H → H is definable in the ambient structure;
- faithful, if only the identity of G acts trivially;
- irreducible, if H is connected and admits no proper nontrivial G-invariant definable connected subgroup.

Prove the following: Let G be an infinite simple group of finite Morley rank that contains no involutions (no elements of order 2). Then G cannot act definably, faithfully, and irreducibly on any connected group H of finite Morley rank, except for the case in which H = G and G acts on itself by conjugation.

For a positive integer $n$, let $S_n$ denote the symmetric group on $\{1,2,\dots,n\}$, and let $d$ denote the normalized Hamming metric on $S_n$, defined by
$$d(\sigma,\tau) = \frac{1}{n}\,\bigl|\{i \in \{1,\dots,n\} : \sigma(i)\ne\tau(i)\}\bigr|.$$
A group $G$ is called \emph{sofic} if for every finite subset $F\subseteq G$ with $1\in F$ and every $\varepsilon>0$ there exist a positive integer $n$ and a map $\varphi : F \to S_n$ such that:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\varphi(1)=\mathrm{id}$;
\item $d(\varphi(gh),\varphi(g)\varphi(h)) < \varepsilon$ for all $g,h\in F$ with $gh\in F$;
\item for every $g\in F\setminus\{1\}$, the permutation $\varphi(g)$ has no fixed points (equivalently, $d(\varphi(g),\mathrm{id})=1$).
\end{enumerate}
Question: Is every group sofic?

Define the following operations on finite words over a finite alphabet. Given a word $w$, the *suffix square completion* derives from $w$ any word $wx$ such that $w$ has a suffix of the form $yx$ with $|y|=|x|\ge 1$ (so that the resulting word $wx$ ends with the square $xx$). Symmetrically, the *prefix square completion* derives from $w$ any word $xw$ such that $w$ has a prefix of the form $xy$ with $|y|=|x|\ge 1$. The *prefix-suffix (square) completion* allows either operation at each step. An infinite word $\mathbf{w}$ is said to be *generated by iterated prefix-suffix completion* from a finite seed $w_0$ if there is an infinite chain $w_0 \to w_1 \to w_2 \to \cdots$ of prefix-suffix completion steps whose limit is $\mathbf{w}$.

Decide whether there exists an infinite word $\mathbf{w}$ over some finite alphabet such that:
(i) $\mathbf{w}$ can be generated by iterated prefix-suffix square completion from some finite seed;
(ii) $\mathbf{w}$ is cube-free, i.e., contains no factor of the form $uuu$ with $u$ nonempty;
(iii) every factor of $\mathbf{w}$ of sufficient length contains a square, i.e., $\mathbf{w}$ is not square-free in a strong sense — more precisely, every sufficiently long factor of $\mathbf{w}$ contains a factor of the form $uu$ with $u$ nonempty.

In short: can prefix-suffix completion produce infinite cube-free words in which every (sufficiently long) factor contains a square?

Consider the Einstein equations with a negative cosmological constant Λ < 0, whose maximally symmetric vacuum solution is Anti-de Sitter (AdS) spacetime. In the spherically symmetric setting coupled to a massless real scalar field, numerical and partial analytical evidence supports the conjecture that AdS spacetime is nonlinearly unstable: arbitrarily small, generic perturbations evolve through a weakly turbulent mechanism that cascades energy to higher and higher frequencies, eventually concentrating enough energy to form a trapped surface and hence a black hole.

Problem. Investigate whether this conjectured nonlinear instability of AdS spacetime — characterized by a weakly turbulent transfer of energy to high frequencies culminating in black-hole formation — persists, is modified, or fails in settings that go beyond spherically symmetric massless scalar-field collapse. In particular, determine the fate of small generic perturbations of AdS in models such as:
(i) vacuum Einstein equations with Λ < 0 (pure gravitational perturbations, breaking spherical and/or axial symmetry);
(ii) higher-dimensional AdS_d with d ≥ 4 under non-spherically symmetric perturbations;
(iii) Einstein equations coupled to other matter models (e.g. massive scalar, complex scalar, perfect fluids, Einstein–Maxwell);
(iv) asymptotically AdS solutions with reflecting boundary conditions but without imposed symmetry.

For each such setting, decide whether generic small initial data lead, after sufficiently long time, to black-hole formation via a turbulent cascade, or whether new phenomena (e.g. globally regular quasi-periodic solutions, stable islands, or absence of cascade) prevent the instability.

Let $f_t : \mathcal{X} \to \mathcal{Y}$ denote a (possibly black-box) predictive model at time $t \in \mathbb{N}$, deployed on inputs drawn from a time-varying distribution $P_t$ over $\mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{Y}$, and let $u_t : \mathcal{Y} \times \mathcal{A} \to \mathbb{R}$ be a time-varying utility function used by downstream decision-makers (with action set $\mathcal{A}$). A causal explanation method is a mapping $E$ that, given a query input $x \in \mathcal{X}$ and the triple $(f_t, P_t, u_t)$, returns an explanation $E(x; f_t, P_t, u_t)$ that purports to identify, in a causal sense, the features or mechanisms responsible for the prediction $f_t(x)$ and/or its utility consequences.

Most existing causal explanation methods are designed under the static assumption that $f_t = f$, $P_t = P$, and $u_t = u$ do not vary with $t$. Formulate and develop a theory of causal explanation methods $E$ that:

1. Remain valid under distribution shift, i.e. when $P_t \neq P_{t'}$ for $t \neq t'$ (covariate, label, or mechanism shift specified via a structural causal model);
2. Remain valid under model updates, i.e. when $f_t \neq f_{t'}$ (e.g. retraining, continual learning, or online updates);
3. Remain valid under changes in the utility function $u_t$ used to evaluate decisions based on the prediction.

Specifically: (a) Propose a precise definition of what it means for an explanation $E(x; f_t, P_t, u_t)$ to "remain valid over time" across such dynamics. (b) Construct an explanation method $E$ satisfying this definition under stated assumptions on the allowed dynamics (e.g. bounded mechanism shifts, smooth model updates), and prove guarantees (consistency, stability, or calibration) for it. (c) Identify impossibility results characterizing regimes of dynamics under which no such valid causal explanation method exists.

Consider the field of causal reinforcement learning, in which an agent interacts with a Markov decision process (S, A, P, R, γ) and seeks to exploit causal structure (e.g., a structural causal model of the transition kernel P) to improve policy learning. A common sub-goal in this area is causal model-based reinforcement learning, where multiple methods independently aim to learn the environment transition dynamics P (for instance, to improve sample efficiency of policy search). At present, distinct methods sharing this same goal are typically validated on bespoke, non-overlapping task suites, which prevents head-to-head comparison.

Problem: Design (or determine whether one can design) a unified evaluation framework for causal reinforcement learning consisting of a finite collection of benchmark environments {E_1, ..., E_n}, evaluation metrics {m_1, ..., m_k} (such as sample complexity to reach a target return, regret, or transition-model prediction error), and protocols, such that:

(a) every causal model-based RL method whose stated objective is learning the transition dynamics P can be applied to at least one common subset of {E_1, ..., E_n} without modification of its core assumptions;
(b) the metrics {m_j} are well-defined and comparable across methods that may rely on different structural assumptions (e.g., known vs. unknown causal graph, observational vs. interventional data access);
(c) the framework discriminates methods in the sense that, for any two methods with provably different theoretical sample-complexity rates for learning P, the framework's metrics on the shared subset of environments reflect this separation with high probability under stated assumptions.

Either construct such a framework and prove properties (a)–(c), or show that no such framework can simultaneously satisfy (a)–(c) given standard heterogeneity of causal assumptions across methods.

Consider the setting of reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF), in which a policy π_θ over an action/response space is fine-tuned using a reward model r_φ trained on a dataset D = {(x_i, y_i^A, y_i^B, c_i)} of human preference comparisons, where x_i is a prompt drawn from some collection distribution P_train, (y_i^A, y_i^B) are candidate responses, and c_i ∈ {A, B} is a human-provided preference label produced by a labeling process L. Let P_deploy denote the distribution of prompts and interactions encountered at deployment, and let Q* denote an idealized "ground-truth" preference distribution reflecting the values one wishes the policy to optimize.

Devise principled methodologies and accompanying theoretical guarantees for constructing the prompt-sampling distribution P_train, the response-generation procedure, and the labeling process L such that the resulting dataset D simultaneously satisfies:

(i) Representativeness: the induced distribution over (prompt, response) pairs in D is close, in a suitable statistical divergence, to the deployment distribution P_deploy, so that off-distribution effects on the learned reward model r_φ and policy π_θ are provably controlled;

(ii) Informativeness/helpfulness for learning: the dataset is sufficiently diverse and well-targeted that training on D yields low excess risk for r_φ relative to Q*, and the policy π_θ optimized against r_φ achieves near-optimal expected reward under Q* with sample complexity polynomial in relevant problem parameters;

(iii) Robustness to bias and low-quality annotation: the procedure is provably robust to sampling biases in P_train, to noisy or strategically adversarial labelers, and to distributional mismatch between P_train and P_deploy, in the sense that the guarantees in (i) and (ii) degrade gracefully as a function of quantitative measures of these distortions.

Provide constructive algorithms achieving these properties together with matching upper and lower bounds, or otherwise characterize the fundamental limits of any procedure attempting to achieve (i)–(iii) jointly.

Consider a Markov decision process (MDP) M = (S, A, P, r, gamma) with (possibly high-dimensional) state space S, action space A, transition kernel P: S x A -> Delta(S), discount factor gamma in (0,1), and a reward function r: S x A -> R that is not given a priori but is learned from human preference data. Specifically, assume access to a dataset D = {(s_i, a_i^+, a_i^-)} of pairwise human preferences indicating that action a_i^+ is preferred to a_i^- in state s_i, from which one fits a parameterized reward model r_phi via a preference likelihood (e.g., the Bradley-Terry model P(a^+ > a^- | s) = sigma(r_phi(s, a^+) - r_phi(s, a^-))). A policy pi_theta: S -> Delta(A) is then optimized to maximize E_{tau ~ pi_theta}[sum_t gamma^t r_phi(s_t, a_t)] via a deep reinforcement learning algorithm.

Design and analyze an exploration strategy (i.e., a rule for selecting actions during training so as to gather informative trajectories, or equivalently a regularizer / intrinsic-reward augmentation of the policy objective) such that, under reasonable structural assumptions on M and on the class of reward models, the resulting learning procedure provably:

1. Achieves sample-efficient convergence to a near-optimal policy with respect to the (unknown) true human-aligned reward, even when the extrinsic reward signal r_phi is sparse (i.e., nonzero only on a set of small measure in S x A) and the state space is high-dimensional;
2. Adapts the amount of exploration to problem-dependent quantities (e.g., reward sparsity, effective horizon, reward-model uncertainty) without requiring these quantities to be known in advance;
3. Avoids unsafe exploratory behavior, formalized by maintaining, with high probability throughout training, a constraint of the form E_{tau ~ pi_theta}[sum_t gamma^t c(s_t, a_t)] <= delta for a given cost function c: S x A -> R_{>=0} and tolerance delta > 0.

Equivalently: provide a principled and general resolution to the exploration-versus-exploitation tradeoff for deep reinforcement learning from human feedback in high-dimensional, sparse-reward MDPs, with quantitative guarantees on sample complexity, final policy quality, and safety during training.

Let $M_\theta$ denote a pretrained large language model with parameters $\theta \in \mathbb{R}^N$ (with $N$ very large), trained on a broad corpus of source code and natural language. For a target domain $\mathcal{D}$ — for example, a specific programming language, a particular codebase, or a particular application area — let $\mathcal{L}_\mathcal{D}(\theta)$ denote a task loss (e.g., next-token cross-entropy or a code-quality metric) on data drawn from $\mathcal{D}$, and assume only a comparatively small labeled or unlabeled dataset $S_\mathcal{D}$ from $\mathcal{D}$ is available.

Design and analyze an adaptation procedure $\mathcal{A}: (\theta, S_\mathcal{D}) \mapsto \theta'$ producing adapted parameters $\theta'$ (possibly via low-rank updates, adapter modules, prompt tuning, transfer learning, or other parameter-efficient methods) such that:

1. The expected loss $\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{L}_\mathcal{D}(\theta')]$ on the target domain is significantly smaller than $\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{L}_\mathcal{D}(\theta)]$;
2. The computational cost of $\mathcal{A}$ (measured in FLOPs, memory, and wall-clock time) and the number of trainable parameters $|\theta' - \theta|_0$ are dramatically smaller than the cost of training $M$ from scratch on $\mathcal{D}$;
3. Performance on the original general distribution is not catastrophically degraded.

Provide an efficient algorithm $\mathcal{A}$ together with theoretical or empirical guarantees quantifying the trade-off between adaptation cost, sample complexity $|S_\mathcal{D}|$, and the resulting improvement on domain $\mathcal{D}$, for the setting where $M$ is a large language model applied to source-code tasks.

Consider a software engineering process modeled as a directed workflow graph whose nodes are activities (e.g., specification, code generation, static analysis, dynamic analysis, testing, code review, post-processing, deployment) and whose edges represent the flow of artifacts (code, tests, traces, reports) between activities. Suppose one or more nodes are instantiated by a large language model (LLM) — a stochastic function that maps a prompt (string) to a response (string) with bounded but nonzero error rate, hallucination probability, and latency. The remaining nodes are deterministic or semi-deterministic SE tools (compilers, type checkers, fuzzers, test runners, CI orchestrators).

Identify and characterize the design patterns and workflow architectures (i.e., the topologies of such graphs together with the protocols governing prompt construction, response validation, retries, and feedback loops) that allow LLM nodes to be embedded in the overall workflow so that the composite process is simultaneously:
  (i) safe — outputs violating specified correctness, security, or policy constraints are caught and rejected before propagating downstream, with quantifiable bounds on the probability of unsafe outputs reaching the workflow's end;
  (ii) efficient — total expected cost (compute, latency, monetary, human-review effort) is minimized or kept within a stated budget; and
  (iii) effective — the composite workflow achieves measurable improvements (on metrics such as bug-fix rate, test coverage, defect detection, or developer productivity) over both pure-LLM and pure-classical baselines.

In particular, give principled architectural patterns for:
  (a) generate-and-test frameworks in which an LLM proposes candidates that are filtered by an oracle or test suite;
  (b) static- and dynamic-analysis pipelines that use program analyses to constrain prompts and to validate LLM responses (post-processing);
  (c) continuous-integration systems augmented with LLM stages.

For each proposed pattern, provide a precise specification, a model of its safety/efficiency/effectiveness trade-offs, and conditions under which it dominates alternative patterns.

Consider a conversational question answering (ConvQA) system modeled as a dynamical learner that, at each turn t of a multi-turn dialogue, receives a user utterance u_t (possibly with conversational history H_t = (u_1, a_1, ..., u_{t-1}, a_{t-1})) and produces an answer a_t drawn from a parameterized policy/model M_{θ_t} with knowledge state K_t (which may include an external retrievable corpus or vector store). After producing a_t, the system may receive feedback signals f_t (explicit ratings, implicit signals, corrections, or environment rewards) and updates (θ_{t+1}, K_{t+1}) using one or more online adaptation mechanisms drawn from: (i) reinforcement learning with reward r_t derived from f_t, (ii) active learning that selects which interactions or labels to query, (iii) continual learning updates that mitigate catastrophic forgetting on a stream of distributions D_1, D_2, ..., (iv) self-supervised objectives applied to incoming dialogue data, (v) retrieval-augmented generation in which K_t is a dynamic index updated online, and (vi) federated learning across distributed clients with local data that may not be shared.

Design and analyze a single unified online adaptation procedure for (θ_t, K_t) that simultaneously satisfies the following requirements, and provide guarantees for it:
1. Accuracy: expected task loss E[ℓ(a_t, a_t^*)] on the current dialogue distribution is bounded and decreases (or stays competitive with the best in hindsight) as t grows;
2. Robustness: performance degrades gracefully under adversarial or out-of-distribution u_t;
3. Privacy: the update mechanism satisfies a formal privacy notion (e.g., (ε, δ)-differential privacy or a federated protocol with no raw-data sharing) across users;
4. Resistance to catastrophic forgetting: for any earlier distribution D_s (s < t) the loss E_{D_s}[ℓ(a, a^*)] under θ_t remains within a controllable bound of its value at time s;
5. Real-time constraint: each per-turn update has bounded computational cost independent of t.

Determine whether such a procedure exists that simultaneously achieves provable guarantees for (1)–(5), and if so construct it together with corresponding regret, forgetting, privacy, and robustness bounds; otherwise establish impossibility results characterizing which subsets of (1)–(5) can be jointly attained.

Consider a conversational question answering (CQA) system that interacts with a user over a sequence of turns $T_1, T_2, \dots, T_n$, where each turn $T_i = (q_i, a_i)$ consists of a user query $q_i$ and the system's response $a_i$. At turn $n+1$, given a new query $q_{n+1}$, the system must produce a response $a_{n+1}$ conditioned on a context $C_{n+1} \subseteq \{T_1, \dots, T_n\}$ (possibly with some turns replaced by summaries or compressed representations). Assume a fixed bound $B$ on the size (e.g., token length) of $C_{n+1}$, while $n$ may grow unboundedly.

Design a dynamic history management policy $\pi$ that, at each turn, chooses how to selectively retain, summarize, compress, or discard earlier turns, producing a context $C_{n+1}$ of size at most $B$, such that:

(1) $C_{n+1}$ preserves information from prior turns that is most relevant to answering $q_{n+1}$ correctly;
(2) $\pi$ tracks evolving user goals and topic shifts across the conversation, i.e., adapts when the current intent diverges from earlier intents;
(3) The expected answer quality of the system, under a reasonable evaluation metric for CQA, does not degrade as $n 	o \infty$ (scalability to prolonged real-world conversations).

Provide such a policy together with theoretical or empirical guarantees on its relevance preservation, topic-shift tracking, and long-horizon scalability.

Consider a conversational question answering (ConvQA) system modeled as a function that, given a dialogue history H = (u_1, u_2, ..., u_{t-1}) and a current user query u_t together with an external knowledge source K (which may be a document collection, knowledge graph, or parametric language-model memory), produces a response r_t. Suppose that either (a) the query u_t is ambiguous, meaning there exist at least two semantically distinct interpretations I_1, I_2 of u_t each consistent with H, or (b) the supporting evidence in K is uncertain, meaning no single answer a has retrieval/inference confidence exceeding a chosen threshold τ.

Design and analyze a response-generation strategy R(H, u_t, K) that, in the presence of ambiguity or uncertainty, may take any of the following actions:
  (i) output a set {(a_j, c_j, e_j)} of multiple plausible answers a_j, each annotated with a calibrated confidence score c_j ∈ [0,1] and an explanation e_j;
  (ii) emit a clarifying follow-up question q* designed to maximally reduce entropy over the set of interpretations {I_k};
  (iii) detect contradictions among candidate evidence pieces in K and report them;
  (iv) decline to answer (abstain) whenever max_j c_j < τ, particularly in designated high-stakes domains D ⊆ {healthcare, legal, ...}.

Formulate quantitative criteria — such as calibration error of the confidences c_j, expected information gain of clarifying questions q*, contradiction-detection precision/recall, and a domain-dependent risk functional balancing helpfulness against the cost of incorrect high-confidence answers — and determine principled strategies R that simultaneously optimize these criteria. In particular, propose and study methods that provably manage ambiguity and uncertainty in ConvQA so as to satisfy these criteria.

Consider the problem of invariant feature learning (IFL) for supervised prediction under distribution shift. Let $\mathcal{X}$ be an input space, $\mathcal{Y}$ a label space, and $\mathcal{E}$ a set of training environments. For each $e \in \mathcal{E}$, suppose we observe i.i.d. samples $(X^e, Y^e) \sim P^e$ on $\mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{Y}$. The goal is to learn a representation $\Phi: \mathcal{X} \to \mathcal{Z}$ and a predictor $h: \mathcal{Z} \to \mathcal{Y}$ such that $h \circ \Phi$ achieves low risk on a broad family of test distributions $\mathcal{P}_{\text{test}}$ that may differ from the training distributions through changes in spurious (non-causal) features, while preserving the invariant (causal) predictive structure.

Beyond the class labels $Y^e$, suppose the learner has access to additional supervision signals of the following types:
(i) a family $\mathcal{T}$ of content-preserving transformations $T: \mathcal{X} \to \mathcal{X}$ that are assumed to leave the invariant features unchanged;
(ii) discrete environment identifiers $e \in \mathcal{E}$ associated with each sample;
(iii) high-dimensional environment descriptors $E^e \in \mathcal{D}$ in some descriptor space $\mathcal{D}$, encoding richer information about the data-generating context than a discrete label;
(iv) auxiliary object or attribute labels $A^e \in \mathcal{A}$ that annotate semantic substructure of $X^e$ beyond the target $Y^e$.

Formulate principled learning objectives and identifiability conditions, in terms of $(\Phi, h)$ and the auxiliary signals (i)–(iv), under which the learned representation $\Phi$ provably separates spurious from invariant predictive structure (i.e., $\Phi(X)$ depends only on the invariant causal parents of $Y$). In particular, determine which combinations of (i)–(iv) are sufficient for such identifiability and out-of-distribution generalization to $\mathcal{P}_{\text{test}}$, and which are not, giving matching guarantees or impossibility results.

Let $a_1 < a_2 < a_3 < \cdots$ be a strictly increasing sequence of positive integers. For each positive integer $n$, define the counting function
$$N(n) = \#\{ i \ge 1 : a_i \le n \}.$$
For an integer $k \ge 1$, let $N^{(k)}$ denote the $k$-fold iterate $N \circ N \circ \cdots \circ N$ ($k$ times).

(1) For a given positive integer $n$, determine in closed form (in terms of $n$ and the sequence $(a_i)$) the smallest positive integer $k = k(n)$ such that $N^{(k)}(n) = N^{(k+1)}(n)$ (i.e. the iterate stabilises).

(2) Assuming $a_1 \notin \{0,1\}$, determine in closed form the smallest positive integer $k = k^*(n)$ such that $a_k > n$.

Investigate and give explicit answers (or sharp asymptotic estimates) for $k(n)$ and $k^*(n)$ in each of the following choices of $(a_i)$:
(a) the primes $2,3,5,7,11,\dots$;
(b) the perfect powers $m^s$ with $m \ge 1$, $s \ge 2$;
(c) the Fibonacci numbers $F_1=1, F_2=1, F_3=2,\dots$ (taken with multiplicities removed so the sequence is strictly increasing);
(d) the Lucas numbers $L_1=1, L_2=3, L_3=4,\dots$;
(e) the Fermat numbers $F_n = 2^{2^n}+1$;
(f) the (nonzero) Bernoulli numerators (or another agreed enumeration of Bernoulli numbers);
(g) the Mersenne numbers $2^p-1$ with $p$ prime;
(h) the Smarandache numbers $S(m)$, where $S(m)$ is the least positive integer with $m \mid S(m)!$;
(i) the van der Waerden numbers $W(r,k)$.

For an integer partition $\lambda = (\lambda_1 \geq \lambda_2 \geq \cdots \geq \lambda_k > 0)$ of a positive integer $n$, the Dyson rank of $\lambda$ is defined by $\mathrm{rank}(\lambda) = \lambda_1 - k$, i.e., the largest part minus the number of parts. For each positive integer $N$ and each residue class $r \in \mathbb{Z}/N\mathbb{Z}$, let \[ R_N(n, r) = \{\, \lambda \vdash n \,:\, \mathrm{rank}(\lambda) \equiv r \pmod{N} \,\}. \] A classical theorem of Dyson (proved by Atkin and Swinnerton-Dyer) asserts that:
\begin{itemize}
\item For every $n \geq 0$, the five sets $R_5(5n+4, 0), R_5(5n+4, 1), R_5(5n+4, 2), R_5(5n+4, 3), R_5(5n+4, 4)$ all have the same cardinality, namely $p(5n+4)/5$.
\item For every $n \geq 0$, the seven sets $R_7(7n+5, r)$ for $r = 0,1,\ldots,6$ all have the same cardinality, namely $p(7n+5)/7$.
\end{itemize}
The problem is to provide explicit combinatorial bijections that realize these equidistribution statements, in the following strong cyclic form:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Construct an explicit bijection $\Phi_5 : \mathcal{P}(5n+4) \to \mathcal{P}(5n+4)$ (defined for all $n \geq 0$, where $\mathcal{P}(m)$ denotes the set of partitions of $m$) such that $\Phi_5^5 = \mathrm{id}$ and such that for each $\lambda \in \mathcal{P}(5n+4)$, the orbit $\{\lambda, \Phi_5(\lambda), \Phi_5^2(\lambda), \Phi_5^3(\lambda), \Phi_5^4(\lambda)\}$ contains exactly one partition of each Dyson-rank residue class modulo $5$.
\item Construct an explicit bijection $\Phi_7 : \mathcal{P}(7n+5) \to \mathcal{P}(7n+5)$ such that $\Phi_7^7 = \mathrm{id}$ and such that for each $\lambda \in \mathcal{P}(7n+5)$, the orbit of $\lambda$ under $\langle \Phi_7 \rangle$ contains exactly one partition of each Dyson-rank residue class modulo $7$.
\end{enumerate}
Equivalently, exhibit a free action of $\mathbb{Z}/5\mathbb{Z}$ on $\mathcal{P}(5n+4)$ and a free action of $\mathbb{Z}/7\mathbb{Z}$ on $\mathcal{P}(7n+5)$, each whose orbits are exactly the rank residue classes.

Let $S_n$ be the symmetric group on $\{1,2,\dots,n\}$, and let $\mathrm{Des}: S_n \to 2^{\{1,\dots,n-1\}}$ be the descent map, defined by $\mathrm{Des}(\pi) = \{i : \pi(i) > \pi(i+1)\}$. A cyclic extension of $\mathrm{Des}$ on a subset $T \subseteq S_n$ consists of a pair $(\mathrm{cDes}, p)$, where $\mathrm{cDes}: T \to 2^{\{1,\dots,n\}}$ and $p: T \to T$ is a bijection, satisfying the following axioms for every $\pi \in T$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item (Extension) $\mathrm{cDes}(\pi) \cap \{1,\dots,n-1\} = \mathrm{Des}(\pi)$;
\item (Equivariance) $\mathrm{cDes}(p(\pi)) = \mathrm{cDes}(\pi) + 1 \pmod{n}$, where addition is taken componentwise modulo $n$ on the set $\{1,\dots,n\}$ (identifying $0$ with $n$);
\item (Non-Escher) $\varnothing \subsetneq \mathrm{cDes}(\pi) \subsetneq \{1,\dots,n\}$.
\end{enumerate}

It is known that for a conjugacy class $C \subseteq S_n$ (a set of permutations of a fixed cycle type), a cyclic extension of $\mathrm{Des}$ on $C$ exists if and only if the cycle type of $C$ is not of the form $(r^s)$ for some square-free $r \geq 1$ and $s \geq 1$.

Problem: For every conjugacy class $C$ of $S_n$ that admits a cyclic extension of the descent map, give an explicit combinatorial description (i.e., an explicit construction of the map $\mathrm{cDes}$ and the bijection $p$) of such a cyclic extension.

Let $W$ be a finite group acting linearly on a complex vector space $V$ of dimension $n$. For nonnegative integers $k$ and $l$, consider the $\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}^{k+l}$-multigraded algebra
$$R_{k,l} \;=\; \mathrm{Sym}(V^{\oplus k}) \otimes \wedge(V^{\oplus l}),$$
the tensor product of the symmetric algebra on $k$ copies of $V$ (giving $k$ sets of commuting variables, one set per copy) and the exterior algebra on $l$ copies of $V$ (giving $l$ sets of anticommuting variables). The group $W$ acts diagonally. Let $I_W^+ \subset R_{k,l}$ be the ideal generated by the $W$-invariants of positive multidegree, and define the coinvariant ring
$$C_{k,l}^W \;=\; R_{k,l} \big/ I_W^+ .$$
Describe the structure of $C_{k,l}^W$ as completely as possible. In particular determine:

(a) its multigraded dimension (Hilbert series) and total dimension as a $\mathbb{C}$-vector space;

(b) its ring-theoretic structure (presentation, generators, relations);

(c) its decomposition into irreducible $W$-representations as a multigraded $W$-module;

(d) a Gröbner basis of the defining ideal $I_W^+$;

(e) free resolutions of $C_{k,l}^W$ as an $R_{k,l}$-module;

(f) geometric or representation-theoretic interpretations (e.g.\ in terms of harmonics, Hessenberg varieties, flag varieties, or related schemes).

The cases of interest include all finite complex reflection groups $W$, and in particular the symmetric group $W = S_n$ acting by permutation of coordinates.

Quivers considered here are finite directed graphs without loops or 2-cycles, with multiple edges allowed. For a quiver $Q$ and a vertex $i$, write $Q\setminus\{i\}$ for the full subquiver obtained by deleting $i$, and write $\mu_i(Q)$ for the quiver obtained from $Q$ by Fomin–Zelevinsky mutation at $i$. An ordered pair of vertices $(i,j)$ in $Q$ is called a covering pair if every arrow incident to $i$ is either an arrow $i\to j$ or an arrow whose other endpoint coincides with some arrow ending or beginning at $j$ in a way that "covers" the neighborhood of $i$ through $j$ (equivalently: every arrow into $i$ comes from a predecessor of $j$, and every arrow out of $i$ goes to a successor of $j$, with $i\to j$ an arrow).

Define the class of Banff quivers as the smallest class $\mathcal{B}$ of quivers such that:
(B1) every acyclic quiver belongs to $\mathcal{B}$;
(B2) if $Q\in\mathcal{B}$ and $Q'$ is mutation-equivalent to $Q$, then $Q'\in\mathcal{B}$;
(B3) if $Q$ has a covering pair $(i,j)$ such that both $Q\setminus\{i\}$ and $Q\setminus\{j\}$ are in $\mathcal{B}$, then $Q\in\mathcal{B}$.

Define the class of Louise quivers as the smallest class $\mathcal{L}$ of quivers such that:
(L1) every acyclic quiver belongs to $\mathcal{L}$;
(L2) if $Q\in\mathcal{L}$ and $Q'$ is mutation-equivalent to $Q$, then $Q'\in\mathcal{L}$;
(L3) if $Q$ has a covering pair $(i,j)$ such that $Q\setminus\{i\}$, $Q\setminus\{j\}$, and $Q\setminus\{i,j\}$ are all in $\mathcal{L}$, then $Q\in\mathcal{L}$.

Clearly $\mathcal{L}\subseteq\mathcal{B}$. The problem: either exhibit a quiver in $\mathcal{B}\setminus\mathcal{L}$, or prove that $\mathcal{B}=\mathcal{L}$.

Define the class of Banff quivers as the smallest class B of finite quivers (directed multigraphs without loops or 2-cycles) such that:
(i) every acyclic quiver belongs to B;
(ii) if Q is mutation-equivalent (in the sense of Fomin–Zelevinsky quiver mutation) to a quiver in B, then Q ∈ B;
(iii) if Q has a covering pair (i,j) — that is, an arrow from i to j that does not lie on any oriented cycle — such that both Q\{i} and Q\{j} belong to B, then Q ∈ B.

Define the class P (introduced by Kontsevich–Soibelman) as the class of quivers Q for which there exists a sequence of mutations transforming Q into a quiver Q' such that the c-vectors of Q' (equivalently, the framed/principal-coefficient quiver) are all negative, i.e., Q admits a reddening (maximal green) sequence; equivalently, the quivers possessing a reddening sequence.

Problem: Either exhibit a Banff quiver that does not belong to class P, or prove that every Banff quiver belongs to class P.

Let $n\geq 3$ and consider a convex $n$-gon with vertices labeled $1,2,\dots,n$ in cyclic order. The (1-skeleton of the) associahedron $\mathcal{A}_n$ is the graph whose vertices are the triangulations of the $n$-gon and whose edges join two triangulations that differ by a single diagonal flip (removing a diagonal shared by two triangles and replacing it by the other diagonal of the resulting quadrilateral).

Fix an integer $k\geq 2$ and a coloring $c:\{1,\dots,n\}\to\{1,\dots,k\}$ assigning one of $k$ colors to each vertex of the $n$-gon. Call a triangulation $T$ of the $n$-gon $c$-colorful if every triangle of $T$ has vertices of at least two distinct colors (equivalently, no triangle of $T$ is monochromatic). Let $\mathcal{F}_n(c)$ denote the induced subgraph of $\mathcal{A}_n$ on the set of $c$-colorful triangulations.

When $k=2$ and $c$ is the alternating coloring on an even $n$, the graph $\mathcal{F}_n(c)$ is the previously studied "flip graph on colorful triangulations." The problem is:

Determine the structural properties of the induced subgraphs $\mathcal{F}_n(c)$ as $c$ ranges over arbitrary color sequences with $k=2$, and more generally as $k\geq 3$. In particular, investigate:

(a) connectivity and diameter of $\mathcal{F}_n(c)$;
(b) existence of Hamilton paths and Hamilton cycles in $\mathcal{F}_n(c)$ as a function of the coloring $c$ (e.g., as a function of the number and pattern of color changes around the $n$-gon);
(c) regularity, vertex-transitivity, and other symmetry properties;
(d) how these properties degrade or change when $k>2$ colors are used.

Provide a structural characterization or classification of these subgraphs in terms of the underlying coloring $c$.

Background. Fix an integer n ≥ 1 and consider n+2 collinear points p_0, p_1, …, p_{n+1} in the plane. A wiggly arc is an isotopy class of simple curves with endpoints in {p_0,…,p_{n+1}} that wiggles above and below the line and never has both endpoints equal. Two wiggly arcs are called pointed if at every common endpoint their germs do not alternate, and non-crossing if they admit representatives meeting only at endpoints. The wiggly complex W_n is the simplicial complex whose vertices are wiggly arcs and whose faces are the sets of wiggly arcs that are pairwise pointed and non-crossing. Its facets, the wiggly pseudotriangulations, are in bijection with wiggly permutations of [2n], and W_n is isomorphic to the boundary complex of the polar dual of a polytope known as the wigglyhedron, generalizing the associahedron and permutahedron.

Problem. Propose and develop the "multi" analogue of the wiggly complex: that is, define a multi wiggly complex W_n^{(k)} depending on an additional multiplicity parameter k ≥ 1, in such a way that:

(a) W_n^{(1)} recovers the ordinary wiggly complex W_n;
(b) W_n^{(k)} is a pure pseudomanifold whose facets correspond to a natural "multi" generalization of wiggly pseudotriangulations and are enumerated by a suitable family of multi wiggly permutations;
(c) the facet-adjacency graph of W_n^{(k)} carries a lattice structure extending the wiggly lattice as a sublattice of an appropriate higher weak order;
(d) W_n^{(k)} is realized as the boundary complex of the polar dual of a polytope (a "multi wigglyhedron") that specializes to known multi-associahedra and multi-permutahedra on appropriate faces.

Establish the combinatorial structure (vertices, faces, flips, dimension, f-vector), the order-theoretic structure (lattice property, congruences), and the geometric/polytopal realization of this multi wiggly complex.

Let G = (V, E) be a finite simple graph and let k be an integer with 1 ≤ k ≤ |V|. The k-token graph F_k(G) of G is the graph whose vertex set consists of all k-element subsets of V, where two such subsets A and B are adjacent in F_k(G) if and only if their symmetric difference A △ B = {a, b} satisfies ab ∈ E(G). A graph H is Hamiltonian if it contains a spanning cycle.

Tchuente's classical theorem characterizes, for a connected graph G on n vertices, when the Cayley-type graph on permutations generated by adjacent transpositions (the n-token analogue) is Hamiltonian. In the spirit of Tchuente's result, provide a structural characterization of the pairs (G, k) for which the k-token graph F_k(G) is Hamiltonian, in each of the following three restricted settings:

(a) G is a tree;
(b) G is a path P_n;
(c) G is a cycle C_n.

That is, determine necessary and sufficient combinatorial conditions on (G, k) for F_k(G) to admit a Hamiltonian cycle, when G is restricted to each of these three families.

Let $G=(V,E)$ be a simple graph and let $\{X,Y\}$ be a partition of $V$ (so $V=X\cup Y$ and $X\cap Y=\varnothing$). Orient every edge of $G$ that is incident to $X$ from its endpoint in $X$ to its other endpoint; edges with both endpoints in $Y$ remain unoriented (or may be oriented arbitrarily). A directed path on three vertices $P_3 = (v_1, v_2, v_3)$ (with edges $v_1v_2$ and $v_2v_3$) is called admissible if either:
\begin{itemize}
 \item its middle vertex $v_2$ lies in $X$, in which case the two edges of the path are alternately oriented (both pointing out of $v_2$, i.e.\ $v_2\to v_1$ and $v_2\to v_3$); or
 \item its starting vertex $v_1$ lies in $X$, in which case the path is consecutively oriented ($v_1\to v_2\to v_3$).
\end{itemize}
Characterize the graphs $G$ together with bipartitions $(X,Y)$ as above for which the edge set $E(G)$ admits a partition into admissible directed $P_3$'s; equivalently, determine necessary and sufficient conditions for the existence of such a partition, and study the associated extremal and algorithmic questions.

Background. A term rewriting system is a finite set of rewrite rules $\ell \to r$ over terms built from a signature of function symbols and variables. A reduction (or simplification) ordering on terms is a well-founded strict partial order $\succ$ on terms that is closed under contexts and substitutions; such an ordering proves termination of a rewriting system $R$ when $\ell \succ r$ holds for every rule $\ell \to r \in R$. Many concrete families of such orderings have been introduced, including:

(i) the recursive path ordering (RPO) and its lexicographic variant (LPO), parameterized by a precedence (a strict partial order on function symbols);
(ii) the Knuth–Bendix ordering (KBO), parameterized by a precedence and an assignment of non-negative weights to function symbols;
(iii) polynomial interpretation orderings, where each $n$-ary function symbol is interpreted as a monotone polynomial $\mathbb{N}^n \to \mathbb{N}$ and $s \succ t$ iff the induced interpretation of $s$ is pointwise greater than that of $t$;
(iv) semantic path orderings and other extensions.

For each such ordering family $\mathcal{O}$, consider the following decision problems:

(a) Orientation problem: given two terms $s$ and $t$, does there exist an ordering $\succ$ in the family $\mathcal{O}$ (i.e., a choice of parameters such as precedence, weights, or interpretation) such that $s \succ t$?
(b) Simultaneous orientation: given a finite rewriting system $R$, does there exist $\succ \in \mathcal{O}$ such that $\ell \succ r$ for every rule $\ell \to r \in R$?
(c) Fixed-parameter version: given the parameters defining $\succ \in \mathcal{O}$ and two terms $s, t$, decide whether $s \succ t$.

Problem. Determine the exact computational complexity (with matching upper and lower bounds) of each of the above decision problems, for each of the ordering families listed in (i)–(iv) and other standard term orderings used to establish termination of term rewriting systems.

An equational theory is given by a finite set $E$ of equations between terms over a finite signature $\Sigma$. The uniform word problem for $E$ asks, given as input any two terms $s,t$ over $\Sigma$, whether $s =_E t$ holds (i.e., whether the equality is a consequence of $E$ in equational logic).

A "rewriting-based decision method" for $=_E$ is, informally, any procedure that decides $=_E$ via some variant of the term-rewriting paradigm. Examples include:
 (a) a finite confluent and terminating term rewriting system $R$ such that $=_R \;=\; =_E$ (a canonical / convergent rewriting system);
 (b) a finite term rewriting system $R$ together with a congruence $\sim$ such that rewriting modulo $\sim$ is confluent and terminating and decides $=_E$, where the matching problem modulo $\sim$ is decidable;
 (c) more generally, any procedure based on (ordered, conditional, constrained, modulo-an-equational-theory, etc.) rewriting that decides $=_E$.

Question: Does there exist a finite equational theory $E$ whose uniform word problem $=_E$ is decidable, but for which no such rewriting-based variant — including rewriting modulo a congruence with decidable matching, or any other reasonable extension of the rewriting paradigm — yields a decision procedure for $=_E$?

A constraint solving algorithm over a signature Σ and a class of formulas (e.g., equational, disequational, ordering, membership, or arithmetic constraints) takes as input a constraint problem and decides its satisfiability in a fixed structure, optionally producing a representation of the solution set. Given a family of such algorithms, each designed for its own signature Σ_i and class of constraints C_i with its own semantics (free term algebra, rational trees, finite domains, linear arithmetic, sort/order constraints, etc.), provide a general framework (combination method) that, from the individual constraint solvers, automatically yields a sound and complete decision procedure for the satisfiability of mixed constraints built over the union of the signatures Σ_i and the union of the constraint classes C_i, together with sufficient conditions on the component theories and solvers (such as stable infiniteness, signature disjointness, suitable model-theoretic or term-rewriting properties) under which the combined procedure terminates and is correct.

Specify:
(a) the syntactic class of mixed constraints handled;
(b) the semantic notion of solution for the combined problem;
(c) the interface requirements imposed on each component solver;
(d) the combination algorithm itself;
(e) a proof of soundness, completeness, and termination under the stated hypotheses.

An allegory is a category equipped with additional structure abstracting the calculus of binary relations. Formally, an allegory is a category $\mathcal{A}$ together with, for each pair of objects $X, Y$, a meet-semilattice structure on the hom-set $\mathcal{A}(X,Y)$ (with binary meet $\cap$), and an involution $(\cdot)^{\circ}: \mathcal{A}(X,Y) \to \mathcal{A}(Y,X)$, satisfying:
\begin{enumerate}
\item composition is monotone in each argument with respect to $\cap$;
\item $(f^{\circ})^{\circ} = f$ and $(g \circ f)^{\circ} = f^{\circ} \circ g^{\circ}$;
\item the modular (Dedekind) law: for all $f: X \to Y$, $g: Y \to Z$, $h: X \to Z$,
$$ (g \circ f) \cap h \;\le\; g \circ (f \cap (g^{\circ} \circ h)). $$
\end{enumerate}

Let $\mathrm{ALL}$ denote the equational theory of allegories, i.e.\ the set of all equations between terms (built from variables, composition, meet, and involution, with appropriate typing) that hold in every allegory.

Given two terms $s$ and $t$ over a signature including composition, meet, and involution, together with variables, a unifier modulo $\mathrm{ALL}$ is a substitution $\sigma$ such that $\sigma(s) =_{\mathrm{ALL}} \sigma(t)$.

Problem: Is the unification problem modulo $\mathrm{ALL}$ decidable? That is, does there exist an algorithm which, given any pair of terms $s$ and $t$, decides whether they admit a unifier modulo the equational theory of allegories?

Let $E \subseteq \mathbb{Z}$ be an infinite subset. A sequence $\{a_n\}_{n \geq 0}$ of distinct elements of $E$ is called a Newton sequence (or simultaneous ordering) of $E$ if for every $n \geq 1$,
$$\prod_{k=0}^{n-1}(a_n - a_k) \;\text{ divides }\; \prod_{k=0}^{n-1}(x - a_k) \text{ evaluated at every } x \in E,$$
equivalently if $\{a_0,a_1,\dots,a_n\}$ realizes the minimal $v_p$-valuation product $\prod_{k<n}(a_n-a_k)$ among all choices of $n+1$ distinct elements from $E$, simultaneously for every prime $p$. A sequence $\{a_n\}_{n \geq 0}$ of distinct integers is called self-ordered (self-simultaneously ordered) if it is a Newton sequence of the set $E = \{a_n : n \geq 0\}$ of its own values.

Known examples of self-ordered sequences include:
(i) $a_n = n$ (the nonnegative integers);
(ii) $a_n = \binom{n+1}{2} = \tfrac{n(n+1)}{2}$ (the triangular numbers);
(iii) $a_n = q^n$ for any integer $q$ with $|q| \geq 2$;
(iv) every infinite orbit $\{b, f(b), f(f(b)), \dots\}$ of a nonconstant polynomial $f \in \mathbb{Z}[X]$ with $f \neq \pm X$, starting from any $b \in \mathbb{Z}$;
(v) any affine image $\{\alpha a_n + \beta\}_{n \geq 0}$, with $\alpha,\beta \in \mathbb{Z}$, $\alpha \neq 0$, of a self-ordered sequence.

Question: Does there exist a "natural" self-ordered sequence of integers that is neither produced by a polynomial-iteration (dynamical) construction as in (iv) nor obtained as an affine image (as in (v)) of one of the three examples (i), (ii), (iii)?

A star operation on an integral domain $D$ with quotient field $K$ is a map $\ast: \mathcal{F}(D) \to \mathcal{F}(D)$ on the set $\mathcal{F}(D)$ of nonzero fractional ideals of $D$ satisfying, for all $a \in K^\times$ and $I, J \in \mathcal{F}(D)$:
(i) $(aD)^\ast = aD$ and $(aI)^\ast = aI^\ast$;
(ii) $I \subseteq I^\ast$, and $I \subseteq J$ implies $I^\ast \subseteq J^\ast$;
(iii) $(I^\ast)^\ast = I^\ast$.

A theory of "projective star operations" has been developed in the setting of projective $n$-space $\mathbb{P}^n_K$, where the function field $K(x_1, \dots, x_n)$ is a purely transcendental extension of the base field $K$. In that setting one studies star-like closure operations on suitable classes of homogeneous (or graded) fractional ideals/divisorial sheaves compatible with the projective grading.

Problem: Extend the theory of projective star operations to arbitrary projective varieties. Equivalently, given a finitely generated field extension $F/K$ that is not assumed to be purely transcendental (so that $F$ is the function field of some projective variety over $K$), construct and develop a theory of projective star operations on appropriate classes of homogeneous fractional ideals or divisorial objects associated to $F/K$, generalizing all of the standard properties (such as the analogues of the $v$-, $t$-, and $w$-operations, the notion of $\ast$-invertibility, and the relevant class groups) known in the purely transcendental case.

Fix $\varepsilon>0$. For an integer $N\ge 1$, classify all sets $A\subseteq\{1,2,\dots,N\}$ satisfying both of the following:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $|A|>N^{1/3+\varepsilon}$;
\item for every prime $p<\sqrt{N}$, the image of $A$ under reduction modulo $p$ has at most $2p/3$ elements (i.e., $A$ occupies at most $2p/3$ residue classes modulo $p$).
\end{enumerate}
In particular, decide whether every such $A$ must be "essentially contained" in the value set of a quadratic polynomial, in the sense that there exists a polynomial $f(x)\in\mathbb{Z}[x]$ of degree $2$ such that all but at most $N^{o(1)}$ elements of $A$ lie in $\{f(n):n\in\mathbb{Z}\}$.

In the Standard Model of particle physics, the gauge group is G_SM = SU(3)_C × SU(2)_L × U(1)_Y, and the matter content of one generation consists of the representations Q = (3,2,1/6), u^c = (\bar 3,1,-2/3), d^c = (\bar 3,1,1/3), L = (1,2,-1/2), e^c = (1,1,1) (plus optional right-handed neutrinos). A Grand Unified Theory (GUT) is a renormalizable gauge theory with a simple (or semi-simple with a single coupling) compact Lie group G containing G_SM as a subgroup, such that:

(i) All Standard Model fermions of one generation embed into a (small number of) irreducible representations of G;
(ii) The three Standard Model gauge couplings unify at some scale M_GUT into a single coupling g_G, with the running governed by the renormalization group equations of the field content of the theory;
(iii) The known low-energy phenomenology (gauge symmetry breaking pattern, fermion masses, absence of observed proton decay above current experimental bounds, etc.) is reproduced upon symmetry breaking G → G_SM.

Determine whether such a Grand Unified Theory exists describing nature: that is, decide whether the electromagnetic, weak, and strong interactions arise as different components of a single gauge interaction associated with a simple compact Lie group G ⊃ G_SM; and, if so, identify the gauge group G (candidates include SU(5), SO(10), E_6, the Pati–Salam group SU(4) × SU(2)_L × SU(2)_R, and the trinification group SU(3)^3) together with its symmetry-breaking pattern down to G_SM that is consistent with all current experimental constraints, in particular the lower bounds on the proton lifetime.

Consider Quantum Chromodynamics (QCD), the non-abelian gauge theory with gauge group SU(3) describing the strong interaction between quarks and gluons, defined by the Lagrangian
  L = -1/4 G^a_{μν} G^{a μν} + Σ_f \bar{ψ}_f (i γ^μ D_μ - m_f) ψ_f,
where G^a_{μν} = ∂_μ A^a_ν - ∂_ν A^a_μ + g f^{abc} A^b_μ A^c_ν is the gluon field-strength tensor, D_μ = ∂_μ - i g A^a_μ T^a is the gauge covariant derivative, T^a are the SU(3) generators with structure constants f^{abc}, and ψ_f denotes the quark fields of flavor f.

In the low-energy (non-perturbative) regime—roughly at energy scales below or comparable to the QCD scale Λ_QCD ≈ 200 MeV, where the coupling g is large and perturbative expansion in g fails—provide a controlled, first-principles derivation, starting solely from the QCD Lagrangian above, of the emergence of:
(a) confinement of quarks and gluons into color-singlet hadrons;
(b) the spectrum, masses, and internal structure of nucleons (protons, neutrons) and other low-lying hadrons;
(c) the residual nucleon–nucleon interaction binding nucleons into atomic nuclei;
(d) the observed properties (binding energies, sizes, level structure) of complex nuclei.

Equivalently, exhibit a mathematically rigorous solution (or systematic, convergent approximation scheme) for the non-perturbative QCD vacuum and its low-energy excitations that quantitatively reproduces the formation of nuclei and nuclear constituents from the underlying quark–gluon degrees of freedom.

Nuclear fission reactors produce a flux of electron antineutrinos arising from beta decays of fission fragments of fissile isotopes (principally U-235, U-238, Pu-239, Pu-241). For a given reactor power, fuel composition, and detector configuration, one can compute a theoretical prediction Φ_th for the antineutrino flux (and energy spectrum) reaching a detector, using nuclear data on beta-decay branches and conversion of measured cumulative beta spectra. Worldwide short-baseline measurements of the antineutrino flux, when combined, give an observed value Φ_obs that is approximately 0.94·Φ_th, a deficit known as the reactor antineutrino anomaly.

Determine the origin of this deficit. Specifically, decide among (and quantify the relative contribution of) the following possibilities:
(a) new physics beyond the Standard Model, in particular oscillation of electron antineutrinos into one or more light sterile neutrino states with mass-squared splitting near 1 eV² and a mixing angle consistent with the observed ~6% deficit;
(b) systematic experimental errors common to the suite of short-baseline reactor antineutrino measurements;
(c) biases or errors in the theoretical reactor flux calculations (e.g., in the beta-spectrum conversion of fission isotopes, particularly U-235, or in summation-method nuclear databases).

Provide a definitive explanation, supported by reactor neutrino data (rate and spectral shape, isotope-dependent flux evolution, and very-short-baseline oscillation searches), that identifies the true cause of the 6% flux deficit and, in case (a), the parameters of any sterile neutrino sector responsible.

The Sun exhibits an approximately 11-year cycle of magnetic activity during which its large-scale magnetic field reverses polarity, producing observable phenomena such as sunspots, faculae, and variations in solar irradiance. Historical records also document extended intervals of strongly suppressed activity (so-called grand minima), the most famous being the Maunder Minimum (approximately 1645–1715), during which sunspots were exceedingly rare. Many other solar-type main-sequence stars (late-F, G, and early-K dwarfs) likewise display chromospheric and magnetic activity cycles with varying periods, amplitudes, and morphologies.

Provide a quantitative, predictive physical/mathematical model of the magnetohydrodynamic dynamo operating in the convective envelope and tachocline of a solar-type star that:
(a) explains the mechanism by which the Sun generates and periodically reverses its large-scale magnetic field, reproducing the observed ~11-year (22-year magnetic) cycle, the equatorward migration of sunspot zones (the butterfly diagram), polar field reversals, and hemispheric asymmetries;
(b) extends to other solar-like stars and predicts how activity-cycle properties (period, amplitude, morphology) depend on stellar parameters such as rotation rate, mass, age, and internal structure, accounting for both similarities and differences with the solar cycle;
(c) identifies the physical cause(s) of grand minima such as the Maunder Minimum, and explains the dynamical mechanism by which the cyclic dynamo recovers from such minimum states and resumes regular cyclic behavior.

The model should be consistent with helioseismic constraints on differential rotation and meridional circulation, with observed surface magnetic flux transport, and with cosmogenic-isotope proxies of past solar activity.

Within the framework of standard cosmology, the cosmological principle asserts that, on sufficiently large scales, the spatial distribution of matter and radiation in the Universe is statistically homogeneous and isotropic, so that the geometry is well described by a Friedmann–Lemaître–Robertson–Walker (FLRW) metric. Under this assumption, the only dipole anisotropy expected in a full-sky number count or flux density map of distant extragalactic sources is the purely kinematic dipole induced by the observer's peculiar motion with respect to the cosmic rest frame, with magnitude and direction predicted by the Ellis–Baldwin formula from the CMB dipole velocity.

Consider the following empirical inputs:
(i) the dipole in source number counts and integrated radio intensity derived from large radio-continuum catalogues such as the NRAO VLA Sky Survey (NVSS);
(ii) the dipole in polarized flux density and degree of polarization from the same radio data;
(iii) the observed large-scale alignment of optical polarization vectors from quasars on scales of order a gigaparsec;
(iv) reported large-angle anomalies in the temperature anisotropy of the cosmic microwave background (e.g. alignment of low multipoles, hemispherical power asymmetry).

Determine whether, when these observations are taken jointly and analysed with proper account of statistical and systematic uncertainties, the Universe on its largest observable scales is genuinely anisotropic in a way that violates the cosmological principle, or whether all such observations are compatible with a purely kinematic origin combined with statistical fluctuations within an FLRW + ΛCDM model. In particular, decide whether the matter/radio dipole excess over the kinematic prediction reflects an intrinsic large-scale anisotropy of cosmic structure.

For a graph H and a positive integer n, let t(n,H) (also denoted ex(n,H)) denote the Turán number of H, i.e., the maximum number of edges in a simple graph on n vertices that contains no subgraph isomorphic to H. Let C_{2k} denote the cycle of length 2k. It is known that t(n,C_{2k}) = O(n^{1+1/k}) as n → ∞. Prove that there exists a constant c = c(k) > 0 such that for every integer k ≥ 2,
t(n, C_{2k}) ≥ c · n^{1+1/k}
for all sufficiently large n. Equivalently, construct, for each k ≥ 2, an infinite family of C_{2k}-free graphs on n vertices having at least c·n^{1+1/k} edges.

A graph property P is called monotone if every subgraph of a graph having property P also has property P. Given a monotone property P and a positive integer n, consider the following random graph process on the vertex set {1,2,…,n}: start with the empty graph, and at each step choose an edge uniformly at random among all edges whose addition keeps the current graph in property P; add this edge, and continue until no further edge can be added without violating P. Let M_P(n) denote the (random) number of edges in the resulting maximal graph.

Determine the asymptotic behaviour (e.g. expectation, and almost-sure leading-order asymptotics) of M_P(n) as n → ∞ for each of the following monotone properties P:
(a) "contains no C_4 as a subgraph";
(b) "contains no K_r as a subgraph", for each fixed integer r ≥ 4;
(c) "is k-colorable", for each fixed integer k ≥ 3;
(d) "is planar";
(e) "has girth greater than k", for each fixed integer k ≥ 4.

Quantum field theory describes matter and the electromagnetic, weak, and strong interactions as quantum fields on a fixed spacetime background, while general relativity describes gravity as the dynamical curvature of a pseudo-Riemannian spacetime manifold (M, g) governed by the Einstein field equations G_{μν} + Λ g_{μν} = (8πG/c^4) T_{μν}. These two frameworks have overlapping regimes of validity (for example, near black hole horizons, in the very early universe, and in strong gravitational fields with quantum matter), yet they are mutually incompatible: naive perturbative quantization of the metric g_{μν} around a fixed background yields a non-renormalizable theory, and semiclassical treatments lead to paradoxes such as the black hole information problem.

Formulate a single logically consistent theoretical framework that:
(a) reproduces general relativity in the appropriate classical, low-curvature limit;
(b) reproduces quantum field theory (and in particular the Standard Model) in the appropriate flat-spacetime limit;
(c) yields finite, well-defined predictions in regimes where both quantum effects and strong gravitational curvature are simultaneously important (e.g. Planck-scale physics, black hole evaporation, cosmological singularities);
(d) is internally mathematically consistent (unitary, free of uncontrolled divergences, and with a well-posed dynamical content).

Additionally, identify observable predictions of such a framework that can be tested by experiments or astronomical/cosmological observations accessible with foreseeable technology, given that direct probes at the Planck scale (~10^{19} GeV) are not currently feasible.

Let $G$ be a finitely presented group with a finite presentation $\langle x_1,\dots,x_n \mid r_1,\dots,r_m\rangle$, and let $F$ be the free group on $x_1,\dots,x_n$ with $N \trianglelefteq F$ the normal closure of $\{r_1,\dots,r_m\}$, so that $G \cong F/N$. The abelianization $N^{\mathrm{ab}} = N/[N,N]$ has the natural structure of a (left) $\mathbb{Z}G$-module, called the relation module of the presentation. Define:
\begin{itemize}
\item $d(N) := $ the minimal number of elements needed to normally generate $N$ as a subgroup of $F$ (equivalently, the minimal number of relators in any presentation of $G$ with generators $x_1,\dots,x_n$);
\item $d_{\mathbb{Z}G}(N^{\mathrm{ab}}) := $ the minimal number of generators of $N^{\mathrm{ab}}$ as a $\mathbb{Z}G$-module.
\end{itemize}
Always $d_{\mathbb{Z}G}(N^{\mathrm{ab}}) \le d(N)$. The relation gap of the presentation is defined as 
$$\mathrm{gap}(G; x_1,\dots,x_n) := d(N) - d_{\mathbb{Z}G}(N^{\mathrm{ab}}) \;\ge\; 0.$$

Prove or disprove: for every integer $k \ge 1$ there exists a finitely presented group $G$ admitting a finite presentation whose relation gap is at least $k$. Equivalently, the supremum of relation gaps over all finite presentations of all finitely presented groups is infinite.

Background. A classical star operation on a domain $D$ with quotient field $K$ is a map $\ast: \mathcal{F}(D)\to\mathcal{F}(D)$ on the set of nonzero fractional ideals of $D$ satisfying, for all $I,J\in\mathcal{F}(D)$ and all $0\neq a\in K$:
(i) $(a)^\ast=(a)$ and $(aI)^\ast=aI^\ast$;
(ii) $I\subseteq I^\ast$, and $I\subseteq J$ implies $I^\ast\subseteq J^\ast$;
(iii) $(I^\ast)^\ast=I^\ast$.
Classical star operations (such as the $v$-, $t$-, and $w$-operations) form the basis of multiplicative ideal theory of integral domains and, via the affine coordinate ring, govern divisorial-type structures on affine varieties; equivalently they apply to purely transcendental finitely generated field extensions $F/K$ through the coordinate ring of an affine model.

Problem. Develop a theory of "projective star operations" that plays, for projective varieties (equivalently, for arbitrary finitely generated field extensions $F/K$ that need not be purely transcendental), the role that classical star operations play for affine varieties (purely transcendental extensions). Concretely, propose a definition of a projective star operation acting on a suitable class of fractional-ideal-like objects (for example, coherent fractional ideal sheaves, or graded fractional ideals of a homogeneous coordinate ring, or birational analogues defined intrinsically on $F/K$) such that:

1. The definition is intrinsic to the finitely generated field extension $F/K$ (independent of the choice of projective model);
2. It specializes to the classical star operations when $F/K$ is purely transcendental and the projective variety is replaced by an affine one;
3. The resulting theory yields analogues of the $v$-, $t$-, and $w$-operations, of divisorial ideals, of star-class groups, and of the standard structural theorems (e.g., the finite-character property, Krull-type characterizations, and connections to Picard/divisor class groups of projective models);
4. It interacts meaningfully with the geometry of projective varieties (e.g., with linear systems, Weil/Cartier divisors, and the Cox ring).

Construct such a theory, or prove obstructions showing that no such theory satisfying (1)–(3) can exist.

Let $[n]=\{1,2,\dots,n\}$. A semigraphoid on $[n]$ is a set $\mathcal{S}$ of triples $(i,j\mid K)$ with $i,j\in[n]$ distinct and $K\subseteq[n]\setminus\{i,j\}$, that is closed under the symmetry, decomposition, weak union, and contraction axioms of Pearl–Paz, i.e.:
 (S1) $(i,j\mid K)\in\mathcal{S}\Rightarrow(j,i\mid K)\in\mathcal{S}$;
 (S2) $(i,\{j,\ell\}\mid K)\in\mathcal{S}\Rightarrow(i,j\mid K)\in\mathcal{S}$ and $(i,\ell\mid K)\in\mathcal{S}$;
 (S3) $(i,\{j,\ell\}\mid K)\in\mathcal{S}\Rightarrow(i,j\mid K\cup\{\ell\})\in\mathcal{S}$;
 (S4) $(i,j\mid K\cup\{\ell\})\in\mathcal{S}$ and $(i,\ell\mid K)\in\mathcal{S}\Rightarrow(i,\{j,\ell\}\mid K)\in\mathcal{S}$.

For a positive semidefinite covariance matrix $\Sigma\in\mathrm{PSD}_n$ of a Gaussian random vector $X=(X_1,\dots,X_n)$, let $\mathcal{CI}(\Sigma)$ denote the set of all triples $(i,j\mid K)$ such that $X_i$ is conditionally independent of $X_j$ given $X_K=(X_k)_{k\in K}$. Equivalently, $(i,j\mid K)\in\mathcal{CI}(\Sigma)$ iff the almost-principal minor $\det\Sigma_{\{i\}\cup K,\,\{j\}\cup K}=0$.

Problem: Characterize, as a function of $n$, exactly which semigraphoids $\mathcal{S}$ on $[n]$ arise as $\mathcal{CI}(\Sigma)$ for some $\Sigma\in\mathrm{PSD}_n$. Equivalently, describe the conditional-independence structures realizable by multivariate Gaussian random variables, and determine the geometry of the corresponding Gaussian CI varieties cut out inside $\mathrm{PSD}_n$ by the equations $\det\Sigma_{\{i\}\cup K,\{j\}\cup K}=0$.

Does there exist a data structure that maintains a finite set S of points in the Euclidean plane R^2 supporting the following operations, each in O(log n) time (where n = |S| at the time of the operation), possibly amortized or expected:
(i) insert a point into S;
(ii) delete a point from S;
(iii) given a query point q ∈ R^2, return a point p ∈ S minimizing the Euclidean distance ||p − q||?

Equivalently (via a standard lifting reduction), does there exist a data structure that maintains a set of n points in R^3 under insertions and deletions and supports extreme-point queries (given a direction vector, return a point of S maximizing the inner product with that direction) in O(log n) time per operation?

A finite projective plane of order $n$ (where $n \geq 2$) is an incidence structure consisting of a finite set of points and a finite set of lines, together with an incidence relation between points and lines, satisfying the following axioms:
(i) any two distinct points are incident with exactly one common line;
(ii) any two distinct lines are incident with exactly one common point;
(iii) there exist four points, no three of which are incident with a common line;
(iv) every line is incident with exactly $n+1$ points, and every point is incident with exactly $n+1$ lines.

Every currently known finite projective plane has order equal to a prime power $p^k$ (with $p$ prime and $k \geq 1$). 

Question: Does there exist a finite projective plane whose order $n$ is not a prime power?

A graph property P is said to be monotone if every subgraph of a graph satisfying P also satisfies P. For a fixed monotone property P and a positive integer n, define the random greedy P-process on the vertex set {1, 2, …, n} as follows. Start with the empty graph. At each step, choose uniformly at random an edge among those edges e not currently in the graph such that adding e to the current graph still yields a graph satisfying P, and add e. Continue until no edge can be added without violating P; the resulting graph is called maximal P-saturated under the process. Let f_P(n) denote the (random) number of edges in the final graph.

Determine the asymptotic behavior, as n → ∞, of f_P(n) (typically, with high probability, or in expectation) for each of the following monotone properties P:

(a) P = "contains no copy of C_4" (the 4-cycle);
(b) P = "contains no copy of K_r" (the complete graph on r vertices), for each fixed r ≥ 4;
(c) P = "is k-colorable", for each fixed k ≥ 3;
(d) P = "is planar";
(e) P = "has girth greater than k", for each fixed integer k ≥ 4.

In each case, give matching upper and lower bounds (up to multiplicative constants, or, if possible, with sharp leading-order asymptotics) for the typical value of f_P(n).

Consider the following family of combinatorial games (games "with a Muller twist", also called comply/constrain games), played by two players who alternate moves; the player unable to move loses.

(A) Blocking Nim. A position consists of N heaps of tokens with sizes (h_1, ..., h_N), where N ≥ 2. On each turn, the player about to move (call them the "mover") first has their opponent announce a "block" for each heap: for each heap i, the opponent names a positive integer b_i (with 1 ≤ b_i ≤ h_i) which the mover is forbidden to remove from heap i on this turn. The mover then chooses a single heap i and removes a positive number of tokens from heap i, but may not remove exactly b_i tokens. Standard play convention applies.

Problem (A): Determine the set of P-positions (previous-player-winning positions), and more generally the Sprague–Grundy values, of Blocking Nim:
   (a) for N = 2 heaps;
   (b) for N = 3 heaps;
   (c) for general N ≥ 4 heaps.

(B) Complementary subtraction games. Fix an infinite set S ⊂ ℤ_{>0} of allowed move sizes. The one-heap subtraction game with set S is played on a single heap; a move removes exactly s tokens for some s ∈ S with s ≤ h. The "complementary" subtraction game uses move set S^c = ℤ_{>0} \ S. Determine the P-positions and Sprague–Grundy sequence of the subtraction games with move set S in each of the following cases:
   (i) S is the set of Fibonacci numbers {1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, ...};
   (ii) S = {n ∈ ℤ_{>0} : n ≡ r (mod m)} for fixed positive integers r, m (modular subtraction sets);
   (iii) S is the Wythoff-type set S = {⌊nφ⌋ : n ≥ 1} where φ = (1+√5)/2 (or its Beatty-sequence complement).

Give a complete characterization in each case, or prove that the Grundy sequence is eventually periodic, and if so determine its period.

In combinatorial game theory, a partizan game $G$ is a game between two players (Left and Right) in which the two players may have different sets of legal moves from each position; the options of $G$ are the positions reachable in one move by Left or Right, and disjunctive sums of games are defined in the usual way (on a move, the player chooses one summand and moves in it). A game is said to have a polynomial-time strategy if there is an algorithm that, in time polynomial in the (succinct) input size of the game, determines the outcome class (i.e., which player wins under optimal play with a specified player to move).

Do there exist partizan games $G_1$, $G_2$, and $G_3$ with the following properties:

(a) each of $G_1$, $G_2$, $G_3$ admits a polynomial-time winning strategy;

(b) each of the pairwise (adjacent) disjunctive sums $G_1+G_2$ and $G_2+G_3$ admits a polynomial-time winning strategy;

(c) the same holds for all options of these games and their sums; and yet

(d) determining the outcome of the disjunctive sum $G_1 + G_3$ is NP-hard?

In combinatorial game theory, a (short, loopfree, partizan) game G is defined recursively by its sets of Left and Right options G = { G^L | G^R }, with the base case 0 = { | }. Every such game has a unique simplest representative, called its canonical form, obtained by repeated removal of dominated options and bypassing of reversible moves. A canonical-form engine is an algorithm that, given games expressed as option trees, computes canonical forms and arithmetic of games (sums, comparisons, temperatures, atomic weights, etc.). A search method, by contrast, explores the game tree of a specific position by traversing moves, typically using techniques such as alpha-beta pruning, transposition tables, proof-number search, or Monte Carlo tree search, in order to determine outcomes or best moves.

Problem. Design and analyze an algorithm for combinatorial games that integrates classical game-tree search with a canonical-form engine, in such a way that:

(a) during search of a position P, subpositions are simplified to canonical form (or to a suitable surreal-number / switch / temperature-theoretic invariant) before being stored, so that distinct game-tree nodes equivalent as combinatorial games are identified;

(b) the canonical-form engine is used to prove cutoffs (for example, by comparing a position to 0, to a number, or to a previously analyzed component) in order to prune the search;

(c) for disjunctive sums P = P_1 + P_2 + ... + P_k, the algorithm exploits the canonical forms of the summands together with mean/temperature information to direct the search and to decide outcomes without expanding the full product tree.

Provide such an algorithm, prove its correctness, and establish nontrivial bounds on its running time or on the size of the search space it explores, demonstrating asymptotic improvement over both pure game-tree search and pure canonical-form computation for a natural class of combinatorial games.

In the setting of two-player combinatorial games with perfect information and no chance, the classical theories of impartial normal-play games (where the four standard outcome classes are L, R, N, P, but for impartial games only N and P arise) and of partizan normal-play/misère games provide structured algebraic frameworks (e.g. Sprague–Grundy theory, Conway's surreal-number-based theory of partizan games). For certain non-standard rulesets, however, a position p admits four possible outcome classes characterized as follows:
- N (Next): the player to move wins;
- P (Previous): the player who just moved wins (equivalently, the player NOT to move wins);
- L (Left): Left wins regardless of who moves first;
- R (Right): Right wins regardless of who moves first.
Identify and define a natural class of combinatorial games whose positions take exactly these four outcomes {N, P, L, R}, and develop a useful general theory for such games—analogous in scope to the Sprague–Grundy theory for impartial normal-play games or to Conway's theory for partizan games—including a disjunctive sum operation, an equivalence relation on positions making the quotient an algebraic structure (preferably a group or monoid), a method for determining the outcome of a sum from data attached to its summands, and a canonical form or simplification theorem for positions. Alternatively, prove that no such general theory exists in a precise sense.

Background and definitions. A short combinatorial game (in the sense of Conway) is a two-player game of perfect information with no chance, in which the two players (Left and Right) alternate moves, every play terminates after finitely many moves, and a player who cannot move loses (normal play convention). Each game position G has a recursively defined game value, and the set of all such values forms an abelian group under disjunctive sum. A game G is called all-small (or "infinitesimal of order zero" in the sense relevant here) when each of its subpositions has both Left and Right options. The nimbers *0, *1, *2, ... are the values of single Nim-heaps, and one defines the nim-value (Grundy value) of an impartial position by the mex rule.

For a (loopfree, short) combinatorial game G, define its nim-dimension as follows. Consider the set N(G) of all positions reachable from G (including G itself) whose game value is a nimber *k for some k ≥ 0. The nim-dimension of G is then the supremum, over positions P reachable from G whose value is a nimber, of the Grundy value of P — measured in a suitably normalized scale so that:
  • a game in which the only nimber-valued reachable positions have value 0 has nim-dimension 0;
  • a game whose reachable nimber values are exactly {*0, *1} (and no larger Grundy values appear among nimber-valued positions) has nim-dimension 1;
  • a game whose reachable nimber values include *2 but not *3 has nim-dimension 2;
and so on; the nim-dimension may be a non-negative integer or ∞.

Two specific games:
  • Domineering: played on a finite subset of the square grid; Left places a 1×2 vertical domino, Right places a 1×2 horizontal domino, dominoes may not overlap previously placed ones, and a player unable to move loses.
  • Amazons: played on a rectangular board with a fixed number of Left ("white") and Right ("black") chess-queen-like pieces ("amazons"); on each turn the player to move first moves one of her amazons any number of squares along a row, column or diagonal (not through or onto another piece), and then from its new square shoots an "arrow" (also along a row, column or diagonal, not through or onto another piece) which marks that square as permanently blocked; a player unable to move loses.

Problem. Determine the nim-dimension of Domineering and of Amazons. In particular, prove or disprove the conjectures that Domineering has nim-dimension 1 and Amazons has nim-dimension 2. More generally, characterize the set of values in {0, 1, 2, ...} ∪ {∞} that arise as nim-dimensions of natural short combinatorial games, and compute the nim-dimensions of further specific natural games.

Let $\Omega\subseteq\mathbb{C}$ be a region, and let $\pi(\Omega)$ denote the set of complex univariate polynomials all of whose zeros lie in $\Omega$ (the zero polynomial included by convention), and let $\pi_n(\Omega)\subset\pi(\Omega)$ denote those of degree at most $n$. A linear operator $T$ acting on a space of polynomials is said to preserve $\pi(\Omega)$ (respectively $\pi_n(\Omega)$) if $T(\pi(\Omega))\subseteq\pi(\Omega)\cup\{0\}$ (respectively $T(\pi_n(\Omega))\subseteq\pi(\Omega)\cup\{0\}$).

Provide a complete explicit characterization (analogous to the Pólya–Schur–Borcea–Brändén type classifications known for closed circular domains and their boundaries) of all linear operators $T:\pi(\Omega)\to\pi(\Omega)\cup\{0\}$ and all linear operators $T:\pi_n(\Omega)\to\pi(\Omega)\cup\{0\}$ in each of the following cases:

(a) $\Omega$ is an open circular domain, i.e., an open disk, an open half-plane, or the complement of a closed disk;
(b) $\Omega$ is an open sector $\{z\in\mathbb{C}: \arg z\in(\alpha,\beta)\}$ or a double sector (union of a sector and its reflection through the origin);
(c) $\Omega$ is an open strip $\{z\in\mathbb{C}: a<\Im z<b\}$;
(d) $\Omega$ is a half-line, e.g., $[0,\infty)$ or $(-\infty,0]$.

Let $n,d$ be positive integers with $d\le n$, and let $p(z_1,\dots,z_n)$ be a polynomial in $\mathbb{R}[z_1,\dots,z_n]$ that is:

(i) symmetric (invariant under all permutations of $z_1,\dots,z_n$);
(ii) homogeneous of degree $d$;
(iii) real stable, meaning $p$ has real coefficients and $p(z_1,\dots,z_n)\ne 0$ whenever $\mathrm{Im}(z_j)>0$ for all $j=1,\dots,n$;
(iv) not identically zero, with at least one strictly positive coefficient in the monomial basis.

Since $p$ is a symmetric homogeneous polynomial of degree $d$, it can be expanded uniquely in the basis of Schur polynomials as
$$p(z_1,\dots,z_n)=\sum_{\lambda\vdash d} a_\lambda\, s_\lambda(z_1,\dots,z_n),$$
where the sum runs over integer partitions $\lambda$ of $d$ and $s_\lambda$ denotes the Schur polynomial in $n$ variables associated with $\lambda$.

Question: Must $p$ be Schur-positive, i.e., must $a_\lambda\ge 0$ for every partition $\lambda\vdash d$?

For a real number x, let ||x|| denote the distance from x to the nearest integer. Determine whether there exists an absolute constant c > 0 such that
    ||log a|| > a^{-c}
for every integer a ≥ 2, where log denotes the natural logarithm. Equivalently, determine whether there exists an absolute constant c > 0 such that
    |a - e^b| > a^{-c}
for all integers a, b with a > 1 and b > 1.
